window.TC4_SEARCH_INDEX = {"built": "2026-11-13", "build": "31fb5180", "docs": [{"id": "al-link-words", "url": "al-link-words", "title": "Link your words to the source", "section": "Align", "status": "shipped", "kind": "feature", "summary": "Click a word in the word bank and then the card it belongs under, or drag it there.", "where": "", "headings": ["Steps", "Place several words at once", "Repeated words are numbered", "Undo a link", "Good to know", "Related topics"], "labels": ["Align", "How alignment works", "Translate", "✓ Mark valid"], "text": "Help › Align Link your words to the source Click a word in the word bank and then the card it belongs under, or drag it there. Each source word has a card, and the words of your verse wait in the word bank above them. Steps In Align , open a verse. Click a word in the word bank (1). The cards get a blue outline, and the word's tooltip says \"Now click the card this word belongs under.\" Click the card it belongs under (2). Or drag the word onto the card. Repeat until your words are placed. The count at the top right of the word bank goes up. Click ✓ Mark valid . See Mark an alignment done . Place several words at once Hold Ctrl (Cmd on a Mac) and click each word you want. The words do not have to be next to each other. Shift-click adds every word between the first selected word and the one you click. Click the card, or drag the words onto it. All the selected words go onto that card in one move. A card always shows its words in the order they come in your verse, wherever you drop them. In the picture, la¹, fe and los land as \"la¹ fe los\". Repeated words are numbered When a word comes more than once in the verse, each copy has a small number: de¹, de², de³ and so on. The number tells you which copy it is, counting from the start of the verse. The source cards use the same numbers, for example Θεοῦ¹ and Θεοῦ². A word that comes only once has no number. Undo a link To undo a link, click the placed word. It goes back to the word bank. To unselect a word without placing it, click it again. Good to know How alignment works at the top of the verse gives a short reminder. You can mark a verse done even if some words stay in the bank, for example words your language adds. If you change the verse in Translate later, the changed words go back to the word bank. See Editing a verse you already checked or aligned . A card click places only the words that show as selected in the word bank. The word bank always matches the verse as it is now. If you changed the verse in Translate, new words are in the bank when you open the verse, and removed words are gone. Every word of the verse is in the bank, including words inside added-text or name markup. Related topics Open the Align tool Align a phrase (merge and split cards) Mark an alignment done Alignment suggestions"}, {"id": "al-mark-valid", "url": "al-mark-valid", "title": "Mark an alignment done", "section": "Align", "status": "shipped", "kind": "feature", "summary": "When a verse is aligned, mark it valid. It counts toward the verses-aligned progress. You can also clear every placed word on the verse back to the word bank.", "where": "", "headings": ["Steps", "Clear the alignments of a verse", "Good to know", "Related topics"], "labels": ["Clear alignments", "Clear the alignments of Titus 1:1?", "Next →", "✓ Mark valid"], "text": "Help › Align Mark an alignment done When a verse is aligned, mark it valid. It counts toward the verses-aligned progress. You can also clear every placed word on the verse back to the word bank. It means \"this verse's alignment is done\", even if some words stay in the word bank. Steps Finish linking the words of a verse, and confirm or reject any suggestions. Scroll to the bottom of the verse and click ✓ Mark valid . Go to the next verse with the list on the left or with Next → . Marking a verse doesn't move you on. Clear the alignments of a verse On the same footer row, click Clear alignments . Confirm in the dialog, for example Clear the alignments of Titus 1:1? It tells you how many placed words go back to the word bank, and that every card keeps its original words. After you clear, placed words return to the word bank as separate cards, and Clear alignments greys out until you place words again. Good to know The count at the top of the list (\"0 of 9 verses aligned\") goes up for each verse you mark. If you edit the verse later, the mark is taken back and the verse needs another look. See Editing a verse you already checked or aligned . Upgrading the Greek or Hebrew text also takes the marks back. See Update scripture texts . Related topics Link your words to the source Alignment suggestions Editing a verse you already checked or aligned Update scripture texts"}, {"id": "al-modes", "url": "al-modes", "title": "Align to the gateway text or the original language", "section": "Align", "status": "shipped", "kind": "feature", "summary": "Align to the gateway words you can read (which already carry the link to the original), or directly to the Greek or Hebrew words.", "where": "", "headings": ["Steps", "Which to choose", "Related topics"], "labels": ["Align", "Gateway · ULT", "Original · Greek", "Original · Hebrew"], "text": "Help › Align Align to the gateway text or the original language Align to the gateway words you can read (which already carry the link to the original), or directly to the Greek or Hebrew words. Either way, the saved links point to the original language. Steps In Align , open a verse. Click Gateway · ULT or Original · Greek . An Old Testament book shows Original · Hebrew . Link the words as usual. See Link your words to the source . Which to choose Gateway · ULT : \"Align to the gateway text you can read — its words already carry the original-language alignment.\" Easier if you don't read Greek or Hebrew. A card can hold a gateway phrase, such as \"of the chosen people\" over ἐκλεκτῶν. Original · Greek or Original · Hebrew : \"Align directly to the original-language words.\" The cards show only the original words. Words you already placed stay where they are when you switch. If the gateway text for a verse has no alignment data, gateway mode says \"The gateway lens needs the gateway source text with alignment data for this verse.\" Use the original-language mode instead. Related topics Open the Align tool Link your words to the source"}, {"id": "al-open", "url": "al-open", "title": "Open the Align tool", "section": "Align", "status": "shipped", "kind": "feature", "summary": "Align links each word of your translation to the original-language word it translates. Open it from Check.", "where": "", "headings": ["Open Align", "On this screen", "Verses you imported with alignments", "Good to know", "Related topics"], "labels": ["Align", "Check", "Gateway · ULT", "How alignment works", "Original · Greek", "Start checking →", "Suggest links", "← All checking tools", "← Back to translating", "✓ Mark valid"], "text": "Help › Align Open the Align tool Align links each word of your translation to the original-language word it translates. Open it from Check. The checking tools use these links to highlight your words, and an aligned USFM export carries them. Open Align Click Check , then Start checking → on the Align card. Pick a verse in the verse list on the left. Verses that are not drafted yet are greyed out. Link your words to the word cards. See Link your words to the source . When the verse is done, click ✓ Mark valid . See Mark an alignment done . To leave, click ← All checking tools at the top left, or ← Back to translating at the bottom left. On this screen Gateway · ULT aligns to the gateway text you can read. Its words already carry the link to the original language. Original · Greek aligns straight to the Greek words. See Align to the gateway text or the original language . Suggest links is off at first. Turn it on to get links proposed from the verses you have already aligned. See Alignment suggestions . Above the word bank, a count shows how many of the verse's words are placed. How alignment works opens a short explanation. Note: Align is for Bible books. Story projects have no Align card. Verses you imported with alignments If a book came from a USFM file that already had alignments, its aligned verses open in Align with the notice This verse already holds an alignment . You can't change those verses in Align yet. tC4 keeps their alignment as it was imported and writes it unchanged into the aligned USFM export. Good to know The source cards always follow the original-language text your project uses. If a stored link points to a word that text doesn't have, the link is removed, its words go back to the word bank, and the verse is flagged for review. Related topics Link your words to the source Align a phrase (merge and split cards) Align to the gateway text or the original language Alignment suggestions Mark an alignment done Choose a checking tool"}, {"id": "al-phrases", "url": "al-phrases", "title": "Align a phrase (merge and split cards)", "section": "Align", "status": "shipped", "kind": "feature", "summary": "When several source words are translated together, merge their cards into one phrase card. SPLIT undoes it.", "where": "", "headings": ["Steps", "Good to know", "Related topics"], "labels": ["Align", "SPLIT"], "text": "Help › Align Align a phrase (merge and split cards) When several source words are translated together, merge their cards into one phrase card. SPLIT undoes it. Use a phrase card when two or more source words become one word in your language, or a phrase translates a phrase. Steps In Align , drag a word card onto the card next to it. The tooltip says \"Drag onto another word card to merge them into a phrase.\" The two cards become one phrase card. Place your word or words under the phrase card. To undo, click SPLIT on the phrase card. Good to know Merge only neighbouring words that belong together. If you had already placed words under the phrase card, check where they are after SPLIT . Click a misplaced word to send it back to the bank, then place it under the right card. Related topics Link your words to the source Open the Align tool"}, {"id": "al-suggestions", "url": "al-suggestions", "title": "Alignment suggestions", "section": "Align", "status": "shipped", "kind": "feature", "summary": "tC4 can learn from the verses you have already aligned and suggest links for the next ones. You confirm or reject each one.", "where": "", "headings": ["Steps", "Good to know", "Related topics"], "labels": ["Accept all", "Align", "Reject all", "Reset", "Suggest", "Suggest links", "Suggestions"], "text": "Help › Align Alignment suggestions tC4 can learn from the verses you have already aligned and suggest links for the next ones. You confirm or reject each one. Suggestions are based on how you aligned earlier verses in the same testament. Nothing is saved until you confirm it. Steps Align a few verses carefully by hand first. In Align , tick Suggest links in the Suggestions row. The row says \"Drawn from N verses you have already aligned\". Open a verse and click Suggest . Check each dashed suggestion and click its ✓ to confirm it. Accept all confirms every suggestion, Reject all turns them all down, and Reset clears them. Good to know Suggestions can be wrong, especially after only a few hand-aligned verses: words may be placed in the order they come rather than by meaning. Check every one before you confirm it, and don't use Accept all without checking. Confirm or reject the suggestions before you mark the verse done. If nothing fits, it says \"Nothing to suggest for the words still in the bank.\" The setting is remembered for each project on this computer. Suggestions use the same words as the word bank, so Accept all places every suggestion you see. Suggestions for Hebrew verses work as well as for Greek. Related topics Link your words to the source Mark an alignment done"}, {"id": "bp-add-book", "url": "bp-add-book", "title": "Add a book", "section": "Bible projects", "status": "shipped", "kind": "feature", "summary": "Add one book, or several at once, to a Bible project.", "where": "", "headings": ["Steps", "Good to know", "Related topics"], "labels": ["Add a book", "Add books from USFM files", "Book", "Choose several at once →", "Create book", "Create {n} books", "Pick one book →", "Start a blank book", "Understand", "← Back"], "text": "Help › Bible projects Add a book Add one book, or several at once, to a Bible project. The English drafting sources come with tC4, so you can start without an internet connection. Steps On Home, click the Add a book tile on your project card. Click Start a blank book . Choose the book in the Book list (1). To add several books, click Choose several at once → (2) and click each book. Pick one book → goes back to the list. Click Create book (3), or Create {n} books . The new book opens in Understand at chapter 1, so you can read the passage and its helps first. To cancel, click ← Back or the ✕. Nothing is added. Good to know A book that is already in the project shows ✓ and can't be picked again. The first step also offers Add books from USFM files : \"Choose one or more USFM files. Each file becomes a book, with the file's text as its draft.\" Practise with a short book such as Titus, Philemon or 3 John. Book introductions can't be drafted yet. Removing a book isn't possible yet either. Related topics Open a book and move between chapters Import a translationCore 3 project Create a Bible project"}, {"id": "bp-export-burrito", "url": "bp-export-burrito", "title": "Export a project as a Scripture Burrito", "section": "Bible projects", "status": "shipped", "kind": "feature", "summary": "Save the whole project, with alignments and checking decisions, as one Scripture Burrito zip.", "where": "", "headings": ["Steps", "Good to know", "Related topics"], "labels": ["+ Add a project", "Check", "Community Checking", "Export", "Open →", "Receive work from a file", "Scripture Burrito (.zip)", "Share on Door43", "← All checking tools"], "text": "Help › Bible projects Export a project as a Scripture Burrito Save the whole project, with alignments and checking decisions, as one Scripture Burrito zip. Use it to keep a safe copy, move the project to another computer, or give it to another Scripture Burrito tool. Steps Open the book and click Check . If a checking tool opens instead of the list, click ← All checking tools . On the Community Checking card, click Open → . Click Export (1), then Scripture Burrito (.zip) (2). Keep the suggested file name or type another, then save. The panel shows \"Saved <file name>\". To cancel, close the save window. No file is made. Good to know The zip holds every book of the project, not only the open one. Open Bible Stories projects have the same menu item. To bring the zip into tC4 on another computer, use + Add a project › Receive work from a file . To put a project on Door43, use Share on Door43 on its Home card. See Share to Door43 . On Linux the save window is the system file window and opens in your Downloads folder. If you can't see its Save and Cancel buttons, press Enter to save or Esc to cancel. Related topics Import a translationCore 3 project Export a book as USFM (aligned or plain) Share to Door43"}, {"id": "bp-export-pdf", "url": "bp-export-pdf", "title": "Export a book as PDF", "section": "Bible projects", "status": "shipped", "kind": "feature", "summary": "Make a print-ready PDF of the open book, laid out with your page setup.", "where": "", "headings": ["Steps", "Good to know", "Related topics"], "labels": ["Check", "Community Checking", "Export", "Export PDF", "Open →", "← All checking tools"], "text": "Help › Bible projects Export a book as PDF Make a print-ready PDF of the open book, laid out with your page setup. The file name carries the book code and today's date. Steps Open the book and click Check . If a checking tool opens instead of the list, click ← All checking tools . On the Community Checking card, click Open → . Optional: set the page setup (paper size, columns, spacing). See Community Checking preview and page setup . Click Export (1), then Export PDF (2). Keep the suggested file name or type another, then save. The panel shows \"Saved <file name>\". To cancel, close the save window. No file is made. Good to know Undrafted verses print as \"[ verse not yet drafted ]\". Undrafted chapters at the start or end are left out. If nothing in the book is drafted, tC4 says so and makes no file. The paper size and spacing aren't remembered. Set them each time. Footnotes aren't printed yet. On Linux the save window is the system file window and opens in your Downloads folder. If you can't see its Save and Cancel buttons, press Enter to save or Esc to cancel. Related topics Community Checking preview Export a book as USFM (aligned or plain) Export a project as a Scripture Burrito"}, {"id": "bp-export-usfm", "url": "bp-export-usfm", "title": "Export a book as USFM (aligned or plain)", "section": "Bible projects", "status": "shipped", "kind": "feature", "summary": "Save the open book as a USFM file, with your word alignments or as plain text.", "where": "", "headings": ["Steps", "Which one?", "Good to know", "Related topics"], "labels": ["Check", "Community Checking", "Export", "Open →", "USFM, aligned", "USFM, plain", "← All checking tools"], "text": "Help › Bible projects Export a book as USFM (aligned or plain) Save the open book as a USFM file, with your word alignments or as plain text. USFM is the standard text format for Bible translations. Steps Open the book and click Check . If a checking tool opens instead of the list, click ← All checking tools . On the Community Checking card, click Open → . Click Export (1), then USFM, aligned (2) or USFM, plain . Keep the suggested file name or type another, then save. The panel shows \"Saved <file name>\". To cancel, close the save window. No file is made. Which one? USFM, aligned includes your links to the original language, for tools that read alignments. Align the verses first. See Link your words to the source . USFM, plain is just your text. Good to know Your project doesn't change. A joined verse span is written as one verse, for example \\v 2-3 . Undrafted verses are written as ___ . The header lines ( \\h , \\toc1 – \\toc3 , \\mt ) contain the book code, such as TIT , not the book name. Edit them if another program needs the name. On Linux the save window is the system file window and opens in your Downloads folder. If you can't see its Save and Cancel buttons, press Enter to save or Esc to cancel. USFM, aligned writes the alignment of each verse whose alignment is up to date. A verse that needs review is written without its alignment, and the panel says Your alignment of these verses is not in the file and lists them. Review those verses in Align and export again. The next export clears the message. Verses you imported with alignments are written exactly as they were imported. USFM, plain and PDF never include alignments, so they never show this message. Saving over a file that already exists replaces it without asking. Choose a new file name if you want to keep the old file. Related topics Export a book as PDF Export a project as a Scripture Burrito Link your words to the source"}, {"id": "bp-import", "url": "bp-import", "title": "Import a translationCore 3 project", "section": "Bible projects", "status": "shipped", "kind": "feature", "summary": "Bring translationCore 3 work into tC4 as a new project, from the project zips translationCore 3 exported.", "where": "", "headings": ["Steps", "If the import button stays grey", "Good to know", "Related topics"], "labels": ["+ Add a project", "Add books from USFM files", "Arrives with its importer.", "Bible name", "Cancel", "Import a project", "Import as a new Bible", "Imported", "Language code", "Look up on Door43", "New Bible", "Review files →", "Scripture Burrito", "USFM files", "Use installed versions", "translationCore 3 project"], "text": "Help › Bible projects Import a translationCore 3 project Bring translationCore 3 work into tC4 as a new project, from the project zips translationCore 3 exported. To start a project from USFM files or a Scripture Burrito, use New Bible instead. See Create a Bible project . Each import creates a new project. Nothing on this computer changes until you click Import as a new Bible . Steps On Home, click + Add a project , then Import a project (1). Choose translationCore 3 project (1). USFM files and Scripture Burrito are greyed out here with Arrives with its importer. Start those from New Bible . Drop your files on the drop area, or click it to choose them. Remove a wrong file with its ✕. Click Review files → . Read the review. A green dot means all is well. An orange dot means you need to act. Choose Look up on Door43 (needs the internet) or Use installed versions (1). Check the Bible name and Language code and change them if needed. Click Import as a new Bible . Home shows \"{name} was imported\", and the new card has an Imported badge. To cancel, click Cancel or the ✕. Nothing is created. If the import button stays grey The name is already used. Type another Bible name. A file is damaged. A red \"This file cannot be imported\" box gives the reason. Nothing can be imported from that file. See Messages that protect your project . Good to know Translation Words Links is pinned to a _twl release (matched by date when looking up on Door43). Languages without a Links repository use the English Links that come with the app. All translationCore 3 zips in one import must be the same language. The \"was imported\" message stays on Home until you close it with its ✕ or restart tC4. To add a book to a project you already have, use Add a book › Add books from USFM files . Related topics Create a Bible project Add a book Project settings Messages that protect your project"}, {"id": "bp-new-bible", "url": "bp-new-bible", "title": "Create a Bible project", "section": "Bible projects", "status": "shipped", "kind": "feature", "summary": "Start a new Bible translation project: blank, from USFM files, or from a Scripture Burrito. You add books right after.", "where": "", "headings": ["Steps", "Start a blank Bible", "Start from USFM files", "Start from a Scripture Burrito", "Good to know", "Related topics"], "labels": ["+ Add a project", "Add a book", "Adds Titus", "Bible name", "Blank", "Books from the Scripture Burrito", "Books from your files", "Cancel", "Checking language", "Code", "Create Bible →", "Drop your Scripture Burrito here, or browse", "Drop your USFM files here, or browse", "From USFM files", "From a Scripture Burrito", "How do you want to start?", "Language name", "Left to right", "New Bible", "Next →", "Receive work from a file", "Right to left", "Script font", "The Scripture Burrito's own versification is kept.", "Versification", "← Back"], "text": "Help › Bible projects Create a Bible project Start a new Bible translation project: blank, from USFM files, or from a Scripture Burrito. You add books right after. \"You'll add books next. Everything here can be changed later.\" Steps On Home, click + Add a project , then New Bible (1). New Bible asks How do you want to start? Choose Blank (1), From USFM files (2) or From a Scripture Burrito (3). Start a blank Bible Click Blank . Type a Bible name , for example Kanuri Bible . Type the Language name . When a matching language appears in the list, choose it so Code fills in (for example French (fr) → fr ). You can still type the code yourself. Choose Left to right or Right to left . Leave Checking language on English. It comes with the app, so you don't need to download anything. Click Create Bible → . The Add a book dialog opens. Continue with Add a book . Start from USFM files Use this when your translation is already in USFM files, one book per file. Each file becomes a book of the new project, with the file's text as its draft. Click From USFM files . Drop your files on Drop your USFM files here, or browse , or click it to choose them. Each file gets a row. A green dot says which book it adds, for example Adds Titus . An orange dot means the file can't be used, and the row says why and on which line (1). Remove that file with its ✕, fix it, and add it again. When every row is a book, click Next → . Fill in the Bible name, language, code and direction as for a blank Bible. Under the form, Books from your files lists the books (1), and a note says which Versification the books' verse counts fit (2). Most projects keep it. Click Create Bible → . The project is made with all its books in one step, and Add a book opens in case you want more. Start from a Scripture Burrito Use this for a Bible translation another tool saved as a Scripture Burrito .zip . Click From a Scripture Burrito , then drop the .zip on Drop your Scripture Burrito here, or browse , or click it to choose it. The form fills in from the Burrito: the name, language and code. Change them if you need to. Books from the Scripture Burrito lists its books (1). A Burrito with its own versification keeps it, and the form says The Scripture Burrito's own versification is kept. Click Create Bible → . The books and any other files of the Burrito come into the new project, and Add a book opens. To cancel at any step, click Cancel , ← Back or the ✕. Nothing is saved. Good to know The name needs at least one plain letter or digit, because it also names the project folder. If a project with that name is already on this computer, Create Bible → stays off until you choose another name. Script font sets the font your translation is shown in. Versification is the verse-numbering scheme; most projects keep the default. If a language code is refused, try the code for your specific language, for example knc for Central Kanuri rather than kau . Each USFM file must hold one book and start with an \\id line. Two files of the same book are refused, and the row names the book. A Burrito that holds something other than a Bible translation, for example Open Bible Stories, is refused and says what it holds. A Burrito exported from tC4 is work for a project: tC4 asks you to open it with Receive work from a file instead. Wait a moment after clicking into a field before you type, then check the name. The first letters can be lost. You can change text direction and font later in Project settings . The name and language can't be changed yet. Related topics Add a book Import a translationCore 3 project Project settings Change the checking language"}, {"id": "bp-open-book", "url": "bp-open-book", "title": "Open a book and move between chapters", "section": "Bible projects", "status": "shipped", "kind": "feature", "summary": "Click a book tile on Home to open it. Inside the project, the book list on the left moves you between books and chapters.", "where": "", "headings": ["Steps", "Good to know", "Related topics"], "labels": ["Opening the project…", "Toggle book list", "Understand"], "text": "Help › Bible projects Open a book and move between chapters Click a book tile on Home to open it. Inside the project, the book list on the left moves you between books and chapters. Each book remembers where you were, so a tile takes you back to the same place. Steps On Home, click a book tile, for example Titus . A book you haven't opened before starts in Understand . In the list on the left, click a chapter number (1). To open another book of the project, click its name in the list. If the list is hidden, click the Toggle book list button (2) next to the chapter heading. Good to know The heading above the text (for example \"Titus 1\") tells you where you are. The percentage under each book shows how much of it is drafted. A large project can show Opening the project… for a moment while tC4 reads its saved work. Related topics Resume where you left off Add a book Understand, Translate and Check — the three modes"}, {"id": "ck-bookmarks", "url": "ck-bookmarks", "title": "Bookmark a check", "section": "Check", "status": "shipped", "kind": "feature", "summary": "Flag a check item to come back to it later, for example to discuss with your team.", "where": "", "headings": ["Steps", "Related topics"], "labels": ["Bookmarked", "⚑ Bookmark"], "text": "Help › Check Bookmark a check Flag a check item to come back to it later, for example to discuss with your team. A bookmark doesn't change the decision. Steps In a checking tool, open the item. Click ⚑ Bookmark . It now reads Bookmarked (1), and the item gets a small flag in the list. Later, click the Bookmarked filter at the top of the list to see every bookmarked item. To remove a bookmark, click Bookmarked again. Add a comment to say why you bookmarked it. See Write a check comment . Related topics Write a check comment Filter and sort check items Decide on a check item (valid, invalid, to do)"}, {"id": "ck-comments", "url": "ck-comments", "title": "Write a check comment", "section": "Check", "status": "shipped", "kind": "feature", "summary": "Add a note to a check item about what you decided or what to ask your team.", "where": "", "headings": ["Steps", "Related topics"], "labels": ["Commented", "Delete comment", "Done", "Hide comment", "My comment on this check", "Understand", "✎ Add a comment"], "text": "Help › Check Write a check comment Add a note to a check item about what you decided or what to ask your team. Use it to record why you decided something, or a question for a consultant. Steps In a checking tool, click ✎ Add a comment . Type your note in the box My comment on this check . Click Done . The item gets a ✎ in the list and counts under the Commented filter. To close the box without changes, click Hide comment . To remove a comment, click Delete comment . Check comments can be deleted; comments in Understand can't. See Write a user comment . Related topics Bookmark a check Decide on a check item (valid, invalid, to do) Write a user comment"}, {"id": "ck-community-checking", "url": "ck-community-checking", "title": "Community Checking preview", "section": "Check", "status": "shipped", "kind": "feature", "summary": "Read the whole book as a printed page, set up the page, and export a copy for reviewers.", "where": "", "headings": ["Preview and export", "Good to know", "Related topics"], "labels": ["A4", "Check", "Columns", "Community Checking", "Double", "Drop-cap chapters", "Export", "Footnotes", "Letter", "Open →", "Page setup", "Paper size", "Single", "Spacing", "Verse numbers", "← All checking tools"], "text": "Help › Check Community Checking preview Read the whole book as a printed page, set up the page, and export a copy for reviewers. Use it to read the book as a whole, or to read it aloud with your community. Preview and export Click Check , then Open → on the Community Checking card. Scroll the preview. Verses that are not drafted yet show as \"[ verse not yet drafted ]\". In Page setup , choose Columns (1 or 2), Spacing ( Single or Double ) and Paper size ( A4 or Letter ). Turn Drop-cap chapters and Verse numbers on or off. Click Export to save a copy. See Export a book as PDF , Export a book as USFM and Export a project as a Scripture Burrito . To go back, click ← All checking tools . Good to know Double spacing leaves room for reviewers to write between the lines. The page setup changes only the preview and the export, not your translation. With Drop-cap chapters on, the large chapter number starts verse 1, so verse 1 has no small number of its own. Turn drop caps off to show it. translationCore does not keep the page setup when you leave this screen. Set it again before you export. Footnotes is greyed out and cannot be turned on yet. Related topics Choose a checking tool Export a book as PDF Export a book as USFM (aligned or plain) Export a project as a Scripture Burrito"}, {"id": "ck-decide", "url": "ck-decide", "title": "Decide on a check item (valid, invalid, to do)", "section": "Check", "status": "shipped", "kind": "feature", "summary": "For each check, select the words in your translation, then mark the item valid or invalid, or leave it to do.", "where": "", "headings": ["Steps", "Good to know", "Related topics"], "labels": ["Invalid", "Leave without them", "Mark this check or unselect the words to move on", "Next", "Previous", "Stay", "The selected words are not saved", "To do", "Translate", "Your translation", "← All checking tools", "← Back to translating", "✓ Mark valid"], "text": "Help › Check Decide on a check item (valid, invalid, to do) For each check, select the words in your translation, then mark the item valid or invalid, or leave it to do. Every item starts as To do until you decide it. Steps Read the highlighted quote in the source cards: the original language, the literal gateway text and, when it is on your computer, the simplified text (UST or GST). Under Your translation (1), tap the word or words that translate it. Tap again to unselect. To select several words in a row, drag across them. The drag selects whole words. Click ✓ Mark valid if the translation is right, or Invalid if something needs fixing (2). Click To do to take a decision back. Click Next beside the marking buttons (3) to go to the next item, or click any item in the list on the left. Only a mark saves the words you selected. While you have selected words that no mark saved, Previous and Next are greyed out (3). Pointing at them says Mark this check or unselect the words to move on . If you go to another check in the list, ← All checking tools , another mode or another project with unsaved words, tC4 asks first: The selected words are not saved . Click Stay to keep them, or Leave without them . Marking an item doesn't move you on, so you can change your mind before you go to the next one. Good to know Each mark is saved right away. Only ✓ Mark valid saves the words you selected, and they are still selected when you come back to the item. Invalid saves no words, and To do keeps the words the check already had. If you change the selected words after you marked the item, the mark is taken away and the item counts as not decided again. Mark it again to save the new words. While a mark is being saved, the words, the marking buttons, Previous and Next are off for a moment. If the mark could not be saved, you stay on the check with your words and a notice. A drag that starts to the left of the first word selects text to copy instead. That shows in the normal blue highlight, not yellow. Previous and Next are on the same row as the marking buttons. On a small screen, a long note can push that row below the window. Scroll down to reach it. Your translation shows your words without punctuation, and a word with an apostrophe can look split (for example \"God's\" as \"God s\"). Your text in Translate isn't changed. To fix the text, click ← Back to translating . See Editing a verse you already checked or aligned . Use the Invalid filter later to find everything you still need to fix. If you see \"This check was not saved. The project's resource changed since this book was checked…\", update the resource first, then check again. Nothing was lost. Related topics Check with Translation Notes Check with Translation Words Filter and sort check items Editing a verse you already checked or aligned"}, {"id": "ck-filter-sort", "url": "ck-filter-sort", "title": "Filter and sort check items", "section": "Check", "status": "shipped", "kind": "feature", "summary": "Narrow the list to what still needs work, and change the order of items.", "where": "", "headings": ["Steps", "Filters", "Sort orders", "Related topics"], "labels": ["All", "Bookmarked", "By category", "By frame", "By verse", "By word", "Commented", "Invalid", "Sort", "To do"], "text": "Help › Check Filter and sort check items Narrow the list to what still needs work, and change the order of items. The list on the left of a checking tool has filters and a sort switch at the top. Steps Open a checking tool. Click a filter. Each one shows how many items it has. Next to Sort , click one of the two orders. Click an item to open it. When nothing matches, the list says \"Nothing matches this filter.\" The item you had open stays on the right. Filters Filter Shows All every item To do items not decided yet Invalid items marked invalid Bookmarked items with a bookmark Commented items with a comment Sort orders Translation Words: By word or By verse . In stories: By word or By frame . Translation Notes: By verse or By category . In stories: By frame or By category . Finish a pass with To do , then fix problems with Invalid . Related topics Decide on a check item (valid, invalid, to do) Bookmark a check Check with Translation Words Check with Translation Notes"}, {"id": "ck-tool-picker", "url": "ck-tool-picker", "title": "Choose a checking tool", "section": "Check", "status": "shipped", "kind": "feature", "summary": "Check lists the checking tools for the open book and how far you have got in each one.", "where": "", "headings": ["Open a tool", "The tools", "Related topics"], "labels": ["Check", "Continue →", "Fix this…", "Needs downloading", "No items in this book yet", "Open →", "Start checking →", "Unavailable offline", "← All checking tools"], "text": "Help › Check Choose a checking tool Check lists the checking tools for the open book and how far you have got in each one. Each checking tool takes you through the whole book once, one item at a time. translationCore remembers your place in each tool. Open a tool Open a book, then click Check at the top. Look at the cards. Each one shows how many items are resolved and which one is next. Click Start checking → on the card you want, or Continue → if you have started. On Community Checking the link is Open → . To come back to this list, click ← All checking tools at the top left of the tool. The next time you click Check , translationCore opens the checking tool you used last. The tools Translation Words : key terms, one at a time. Translation Notes : the places a translation note points to. Align : link your words to the original language. This card counts drafted verses, so a book with nothing drafted says No items in this book yet . Community Checking : read the whole book as a printed page and export it for reviewers. A story project has no Align card. If a card says Needs downloading or Unavailable offline , click Fix this… . See Fix a missing resource . The other tools still work while you fix it. Note: Only drafted verses can be checked. Draft first, then come back. Related topics Check with Translation Words Check with Translation Notes Open the Align tool Community Checking preview Decide on a check item (valid, invalid, to do) Fix a missing resource"}, {"id": "ck-translation-notes", "url": "ck-translation-notes", "title": "Check with Translation Notes", "section": "Check", "status": "shipped", "kind": "feature", "summary": "Go through the hard places in the book, such as figures of speech or abstract nouns, and check how you translated each one.", "where": "", "headings": ["Check a note", "The source cards", "Find your way", "Related topics"], "labels": ["All", "Bookmarked", "By category", "By verse", "Check", "Commented", "GST", "Gateway · UST", "Gateway · unfoldingWord Literal Text", "Invalid", "Learn more", "Next →", "Sort", "Start checking →", "To do", "Translation Notes", "What to check", "Your translation", "← Back to translating", "✓ Mark valid"], "text": "Help › Check Check with Translation Notes Go through the hard places in the book, such as figures of speech or abstract nouns, and check how you translated each one. Each item is a note that links to a Translation Academy lesson. Other notes stay in the helps panel. Check a note Click Check , then Start checking → on the Translation Notes card. Read What to check . The words it is about are highlighted in the source cards below: the original language, the literal gateway text (for example Gateway · unfoldingWord Literal Text ) and the simplified text ( Gateway · UST , or GST in some gateway languages). To learn more about the issue, click Learn more under the note. The Translation Academy lesson opens in a panel on the right. Close it with ✕. Under Your translation , tap the word or words that translate the highlighted quote. Choose ✓ Mark valid , Invalid or To do . See Decide on a check item . Click Next → beside the marking buttons to go to the next item. Marking an item does not move you on. To stop, click ← Back to translating at the bottom left. The source cards The simplified card shows the gateway language's simplified Bible: the UST, or a GST such as the Indonesian one. Its words for the quote are highlighted too. It appears only when that simplified text is on your computer. tC4 does not show the English UST in place of a missing GST. See Download source texts . Open Bible Stories checks show the gateway story only. Find your way Sort By verse goes through the book in order. By category puts all the notes about one issue together, for example every metaphor. The filters show All , To do , Invalid , Bookmarked or Commented items. See Filter and sort check items . The notes are in your project's checking language. See Change the checking language . If you change a verse after you decided on it, translationCore asks you to look at that decision again. Nothing is lost. Related topics Choose a checking tool Decide on a check item (valid, invalid, to do) Filter and sort check items Write a check comment Translation notes and Translation Academy articles Change the checking language"}, {"id": "ck-translation-words", "url": "ck-translation-words", "title": "Check with Translation Words", "section": "Check", "status": "shipped", "kind": "feature", "summary": "Go through every key term in the book, such as God, grace or apostle, and check how you translated each one.", "where": "", "headings": ["Check a term", "The source cards", "Find your way", "Good to know", "Related topics"], "labels": ["All", "Bookmarked", "By verse", "By word", "Check", "Commented", "Invalid", "Learn more", "Next →", "Sort", "Start checking →", "To do", "Translation Words", "Your translation", "← Back to translating", "⚑ Bookmark", "✎ Add a comment", "✓ Mark valid"], "text": "Help › Check Check with Translation Words Go through every key term in the book, such as God, grace or apostle, and check how you translated each one. Use it to make sure each term is translated the right way, and the same way everywhere it appears. Check a term Click Check , then Start checking → on the Translation Words card. Read the term at the top. It is highlighted in the source cards: the original language, the literal gateway text and the simplified text (UST or GST). Click Learn more to read what it means. The article opens in a panel on the right. Close it with ✕. Under Your translation , tap the word or words that translate the highlighted term. Choose ✓ Mark valid , Invalid or To do . See Decide on a check item . Click Next → beside the marking buttons to go to the next item. Marking an item does not move you on. To stop, click ← Back to translating at the bottom left. Next time, Check opens Translation Words where you left it. The source cards The simplified card shows the gateway language's simplified Bible: the UST, or a GST such as the Indonesian one. Its words for the quote are highlighted too. It appears only when that simplified text is on your computer. tC4 does not show the English UST in place of a missing GST. See Download source texts . Open Bible Stories checks show the gateway story only. Find your way Sort By word puts every place a term is used together, so you can see whether you translated \"grace\" the same way each time. By verse goes through the book in order. The filters show All , To do , Invalid , Bookmarked or Commented items. See Filter and sort check items . ⚑ Bookmark marks an item to come back to. ✎ Add a comment leaves a note for your team. See Bookmark a check and Write a check comment . Note: A verse that is not drafted yet cannot be checked. Draft it in Translate first. Good to know The check list comes from a Translation Words Links release (for English, en_twl that ships with the app). The articles come from Translation Words ( en_tw ). Some books have no Translation Words check yet (for example Ezekiel), until a Links release includes them. If a link points to an article your checking language does not have, that item is hidden and does not count in progress. Related topics Choose a checking tool Decide on a check item (valid, invalid, to do) Filter and sort check items Bookmark a check Write a check comment Word links (Translation Words)"}, {"id": "gs-home", "url": "gs-home", "title": "The Home screen", "section": "Getting started", "status": "shipped", "kind": "feature", "summary": "Home lists your projects. From here you open a book, go back to where you stopped, or add a project.", "where": "", "headings": ["Open a book", "Add a project", "Change a project", "The account menu", "Related topics"], "labels": ["+ Add a project", "About translationCore", "Add a book", "App language", "Help and guides", "Import a project", "New Bible", "New Open Bible Stories", "Open a project from Door43", "Project settings", "Receive work from a file", "Report a problem", "Resume →", "Save or receive work by USB", "Settings", "Share on Door43", "Sign in to Door43", "Understand", "Use the internet"], "text": "Help › Getting started The Home screen Home lists your projects. From here you open a book, go back to where you stopped, or add a project. Open a book Click a book tile on a project card. A book you have not opened before starts in Understand . A book you have worked in opens where you left it. To come back to Home, click the translationCore logo at the top left. To go straight back to the verse you last edited, click the blue banner or Resume → . See Resume where you left off . The project you used last is always at the top of the list. The line under a project's name shows its language code, how many books it has, and whether it is only on this computer or shared on Door43. Add a project Click + Add a project , then choose one of the five cards. New Bible starts a new Bible translation. See Create a Bible project . New Open Bible Stories starts the 50 stories. See Start an Open Bible Stories project . Import a project brings in a translationCore 3 project. See Import a translationCore 3 project . To start from USFM files or a Scripture Burrito, choose New Bible . Open a project from Door43 opens a Bible your team shares on Door43. See Working as a team on several computers . Receive work from a file opens a teammate's exchange file from a USB stick. To close the dialog without choosing, press Esc or click ✕. Change a project Settings on the card opens Project settings : the checking language, text direction and script font. See Project settings . Add a book adds another book to the project. Share on Door43 uploads a copy of the project to Door43. See Share to Door43 . Save or receive work by USB saves your work to a file, or reads a teammate's, for teams that work without the internet. The account menu Click the round person icon at the top right. The menu is the same on Home and inside a project. Sign in to Door43 so you can share projects. Use the internet turns the internet on or off for translationCore. While it is off, a step that needs the internet asks first. Help and guides opens the offline help that ships with the app. You can browse and search with no internet. App language changes the language of the menus and buttons. See Change the app language . Report a problem opens a form that sends a message to the unfoldingWord help desk. About translationCore shows the version, the copyright and trademark line, the license and the third-party licenses. Related topics Resume where you left off Create a Bible project Start an Open Bible Stories project Import a translationCore 3 project Project settings Change the app language Share to Door43"}, {"id": "gs-install", "url": "gs-install", "title": "Install and start tC4", "section": "Getting started", "status": "partial", "kind": "feature", "summary": "Download translationCore 4 for your computer, install or unpack it, and start it. It opens on the Home screen.", "where": "", "headings": ["Steps", "Good to know", "If it doesn't start", "Related topics"], "labels": ["+ Add a project", "Your projects"], "text": "Help › Getting started Install and start tC4 Download translationCore 4 for your computer, install or unpack it, and start it. It opens on the Home screen. The download already has the source texts, the translation helps and the story pictures, so you can start without the internet. Steps Get the file for your computer from the tC4 releases page : the .exe for Windows, the .pkg for macOS (Apple silicon), or the .zip for Linux. Install it: Windows: run the .exe . If Windows warns about an unknown app, choose More info , then Run anyway . Start tC4 from the Start menu. macOS: open the .pkg . If macOS blocks it, open System Settings › Privacy & Security and choose Open Anyway . Then start translationCore 4 from Applications. Linux: unpack the zip, open a terminal in the translationCore4 folder and run ./start-tc4.sh . The first time, a small window says \"Preparing translationCore4\" and counts the resources, for example \"Preparing resources: 2 of 16\". Wait until it finishes. This can take a minute on a slow computer. Later starts are fast; the window shows only for a moment. tC4 opens on Home with the heading Your projects . Click + Add a project to start your first project. See The Home screen . The warnings in step 2 appear because the installers are not signed yet. They only appear the first time. Good to know Installing a newer build: install it over the one you have; you don't need to remove the old one first. Your projects and settings stay where they are. On macOS, if translationCore 4 is already on the Mac, the installer lists the copies it found and asks Replace it with this version? Your projects and settings stay where they are. Click Replace . Cancel (or no answer for 5 minutes) installs nothing, and the installer then says the installation failed. If an older copy can't be removed, the installer names it so you can move it to the Trash. The tC4 4.0 beta ( translationCore4 ) is a different app and stays. On Windows, Setup installs over the older build in the same folder and removes the older build's program files first. The window's menu bar has Edit , View and Window . Window › Reload reloads the screen. If it doesn't start Windows says \"The backend could not be started\": install the Microsoft Visual C++ Redistributable (x64), then start tC4 again. Nothing new opens: only one copy of tC4 runs at a time. Starting it again brings back the window that is already open. \"translationCore4 could not start … Bundled archive checksum mismatch\": the download is damaged. Click OK, download it again and start the new copy. You closed tC4 while it was preparing: start it again. It continues and cleans up. Linux prints a line about \"--no-sandbox\": that is normal for the zip. tC4 is still a pre-release. Keep a copy of important work elsewhere. Related topics The Home screen Import a translationCore 3 project Where your projects are stored Windows, macOS and Linux notes"}, {"id": "gs-modes", "url": "gs-modes", "title": "Understand, Translate and Check — the three modes", "section": "Getting started", "status": "shipped", "kind": "feature", "summary": "Every open project has three modes. Understand the passage, Translate it, then Check it — in any order.", "where": "", "headings": ["Steps", "What each mode is for", "Good to know", "Related topics"], "labels": ["Check", "Community Checking", "Translate", "Understand", "← All checking tools"], "text": "Help › Getting started Understand, Translate and Check — the three modes Every open project has three modes. Understand the passage, Translate it, then Check it — in any order. Your place in the book stays the same when you switch, and Open Bible Stories projects have the same three modes. Steps Open a book or story from Home. Click Understand , Translate or Check in the middle of the top bar. The one you are in is filled in. What each mode is for Understand : read the passage with its notes, word links and questions, and write your own comments. See Read a passage before you translate . Translate : draft your translation beside the source texts. See The Translate screen . Check : check your draft with Translation Notes and Translation Words, align words, and preview the book. See Choose a checking tool . Many teams go in that order, but you can switch at any time. Good to know A book or story you open for the first time starts in Understand . After that, tC4 opens it in the mode you used last. Check goes back to the checking tool you used last. To pick another one, click ← All checking tools at the top left. Switching modes saves a checkpoint if anything changed. See Saving and checkpoints . Exporting is not a separate mode. It is in Check › Community Checking . See Export a book as PDF . Related topics Read a passage before you translate The Translate screen Choose a checking tool Switch project or go back Home"}, {"id": "gs-resume", "url": "gs-resume", "title": "Resume where you left off", "section": "Getting started", "status": "shipped", "kind": "feature", "summary": "The banner at the top of Home takes you back to the last place you changed something, in the mode you were using.", "where": "", "headings": ["Steps", "Good to know", "Related topics"], "labels": ["Check", "Resume →", "Translate", "Understand", "Your projects"], "text": "Help › Getting started Resume where you left off The banner at the top of Home takes you back to the last place you changed something, in the mode you were using. It shows the project, the book and chapter (or the story), and the start of what you last changed. Steps On Home, find the dark blue banner under Your projects . Click the banner or its Resume → button. tC4 opens that book and chapter in the mode you used last: Understand , Translate , or Check with the same checking tool. Good to know The banner follows your last change, not the last book you opened. Opening a book just to read it doesn't move the banner. A checking decision counts as a change, so after you mark an item in Check , Resume takes you back to that checking tool. Each book tile and story tile on Home also remembers where you were in that book or story. For a story, Resume opens the story at the top. Scroll down to the frame named in the banner. The quote is shortened, so it can stop in the middle of a word. Resume is kept on this computer only. It does not follow you to another computer. Related topics The Home screen Understand, Translate and Check — the three modes Switch project or go back Home"}, {"id": "gs-save", "url": "gs-save", "title": "Saving and checkpoints", "section": "Getting started", "status": "shipped", "kind": "feature", "summary": "tC4 saves as you work. The indicator at the top right shows whether your last change was saved, and offers Retry if it wasn't.", "where": "", "headings": ["Steps", "What the indicator can say", "Checkpoints", "Related topics"], "labels": ["Checkpoint not saved", "Retry", "Save failed — retry", "Save verse", "Saved", "Saving…", "Unsaved changes"], "text": "Help › Getting started Saving and checkpoints tC4 saves as you work. The indicator at the top right shows whether your last change was saved, and offers Retry if it wasn't. There is no Save button for the project: each verse, check decision, alignment or comment is saved when you finish it. Steps Finish an edit, for example click Save verse after drafting a verse. Look at the top right. Saving… changes to Saved when your work is stored. If it shows Save failed — retry , click Retry . If Retry keeps failing, copy your text somewhere safe before you close tC4. See When a save fails . What the indicator can say Indicator Meaning Saved Everything you did is saved. Saving… A save is running. Unsaved changes An editor is still open with text that is not saved yet. Save failed — retry A save did not work. Click Retry . Checkpoint not saved Your work is saved, but the project checkpoint did not finish. Click Retry . Checkpoints A checkpoint is a saved version of the whole project. tC4 makes one when you change mode or leave the project, if something changed. If a checkpoint fails as you leave, Home shows a warning; open the project again and tC4 tries again. Before you close tC4, check that the indicator says Saved . Related topics When a save fails If the app closed unexpectedly or the window went blank Understand, Translate and Check — the three modes"}, {"id": "gs-switch-project", "url": "gs-switch-project", "title": "Switch project or go back Home", "section": "Getting started", "status": "shipped", "kind": "feature", "summary": "Click the project name at the top left to leave the project and go back to Home, where you can open another project or book.", "where": "", "headings": ["Steps", "Related topics"], "labels": ["Switch project", "Translate", "Understand"], "text": "Help › Getting started Switch project or go back Home Click the project name at the top left to leave the project and go back to Home, where you can open another project or book. The top left always shows the project you have open, with its initials, name and language code. Steps With a project open, click the project name at the top left. Its tooltip says Switch project . Home opens. If you changed anything, tC4 saves a checkpoint as you leave. Click a book or story tile in any project to open it. To move to another book in the same project, you don't need Home: use the book list on the left of Translate or Understand . See Open a book and move between chapters . Related topics The Home screen Resume where you left off Open a book and move between chapters"}, {"id": "obs-check", "url": "obs-check", "title": "Check a story", "section": "Open Bible Stories", "status": "shipped", "kind": "feature", "summary": "Check a story with Translation Notes and Translation Words, frame by frame.", "where": "", "headings": ["Steps", "Good to know", "Related topics"], "labels": ["By frame", "Check", "Invalid", "Sort", "Start checking →", "Translation Notes", "Translation Words", "← All checking tools", "✓ Mark valid"], "text": "Help › Open Bible Stories Check a story Check a story with Translation Notes and Translation Words, frame by frame. It works like checking a Bible book , for the open story. Steps Open a story and click Check . If a tool opens instead of the list, click ← All checking tools . Click Start checking → on Translation Words or Translation Notes . Next to Sort , click By frame (1) to go through the story in order. Tap the words in your translation that match the highlighted word, then click ✓ Mark valid or Invalid (2). Good to know Item 1:0 is the story title. Frames are 1:1, 1:2 and so on. An undrafted frame says \"This frame is not drafted yet. Draft it first, then return to check.\" Stories have no alignment. Bookmarks, comments and filters work as for Bible books. Related topics Decide on a check item (valid, invalid, to do) Story preview and export Translate a story"}, {"id": "obs-community-checking", "url": "obs-community-checking", "title": "Story preview and export", "section": "Open Bible Stories", "status": "shipped", "kind": "feature", "summary": "Preview a story as a page with its pictures, choose the layout, and export it for reviewers.", "where": "", "headings": ["Steps", "Good to know", "Related topics"], "labels": ["Check", "Community Checking", "Export", "Open →", "Pictures", "Pictures above", "Pictures wrapped", "← All checking tools"], "text": "Help › Open Bible Stories Story preview and export Preview a story as a page with its pictures, choose the layout, and export it for reviewers. Steps Open a story and click Check . If a tool opens instead of the list, click ← All checking tools . On Community Checking , click Open → . Choose Pictures above or Pictures wrapped (1), and turn Pictures on or off (2). Read the story aloud with your community. To make a copy for reviewers, click Export . See Export stories as PDF or Markdown . Good to know Pictures wrapped puts a small picture beside each frame, so more fits on a page. Undrafted frames show as one line, such as \"[ frames 3–16 not yet drafted ]\", without pictures. Stories have no spacing or paper-size choice. The story PDF is A4, and its first page has only the title. Related topics Export stories as PDF or Markdown Check a story Community Checking preview"}, {"id": "obs-create", "url": "obs-create", "title": "Start an Open Bible Stories project", "section": "Open Bible Stories", "status": "shipped", "kind": "feature", "summary": "Create a project for translating all fifty Open Bible Stories into your language.", "where": "", "headings": ["Steps", "Good to know", "Related topics"], "labels": ["+ Add a project", "Cancel", "Checking language", "Code", "Create stories →", "Language name", "New Open Bible Stories", "Project name", "Text direction", "Understand"], "text": "Help › Open Bible Stories Start an Open Bible Stories project Create a project for translating all fifty Open Bible Stories into your language. Open Bible Stories are 50 short Bible stories, each told in numbered frames with a picture. English stories and helps come with the app. Steps On Home, click + Add a project , then New Open Bible Stories . Type a Project name , the Language name and its Code . Choose the Text direction . Leave Checking language on English. Click Create stories → . The new card appears on Home. Click a story tile (1) to open it. A new story opens in Understand . To cancel, click Cancel or the ✕. Nothing is saved. Good to know Wait a moment after clicking into a field before you type, then check what you typed. The first letters can be lost. You can change text direction, font and checking language later in Project settings . The name and language can't be changed yet. Related topics Find a story on Home Translate a story Project settings"}, {"id": "obs-export-markdown-pdf", "url": "obs-export-markdown-pdf", "title": "Export stories as PDF or Markdown", "section": "Open Bible Stories", "status": "shipped", "kind": "feature", "summary": "Save a printable PDF of your stories, a Story Markdown zip, or a Scripture Burrito.", "where": "", "headings": ["Steps", "Good to know", "Related topics"], "labels": ["Check", "Community Checking", "Export", "Open →", "Pictures"], "text": "Help › Open Bible Stories Export stories as PDF or Markdown Save a printable PDF of your stories, a Story Markdown zip, or a Scripture Burrito. Export PDF: a reading copy for reviewers, with or without pictures. Story Markdown (.zip): the story files for other Open Bible Stories tools. Scripture Burrito (.zip): the whole project. See Export a project as a Scripture Burrito . Steps Open a story and click Check , then Open → on Community Checking . Optional: choose the layout and pictures. See Story preview and export . Click Export (1), then pick a format (2). Keep the suggested file name or type another, then save. The panel shows \"Saved <file name>\". To cancel, close the save window. No file is made. Good to know Draft at least the title or one frame before you export a PDF. To save a second PDF with another layout, change the layout, export again and give it a new name. If you see \"The app could not print the PDF with its pictures…\", turn Pictures off and export again. On Linux the save window opens in your Downloads folder. If you can't see its buttons, press Enter to save or Esc to cancel. Related topics Story preview and export Export a project as a Scripture Burrito Messages that protect your project"}, {"id": "obs-story-tiles", "url": "obs-story-tiles", "title": "Find a story on Home", "section": "Open Bible Stories", "status": "shipped", "kind": "feature", "summary": "The stories card shows three stories. Show all 50 to pick any story.", "where": "", "headings": ["Steps", "Good to know", "Related topics"], "labels": ["1 conflict · Review", "OBS", "Resume →", "Review", "Show all 50 stories →", "Show recent stories only →"], "text": "Help › Open Bible Stories Find a story on Home The stories card shows three stories. Show all 50 to pick any story. Each story is a tile, like a book tile in a Bible project. It shows the story number, title and percent drafted. Steps On Home, find your stories card. It has the OBS badge. Click a tile to open that story, or click Show all 50 stories → (1). Click any story in the grid. To fold the grid again, click Show recent stories only → at the bottom of the card. Good to know Resume → at the top of Home reopens the last story you edited. After a sync that finds frame conflicts, a story card can show an amber 1 conflict · Review chip. Open Review to resolve each frame. Related topics Start an Open Bible Stories project Translate a story Resume where you left off"}, {"id": "obs-translate", "url": "obs-translate", "title": "Translate a story", "section": "Open Bible Stories", "status": "shipped", "kind": "feature", "summary": "Draft the story title, each frame and the reference line, with the picture and the English story beside you.", "where": "", "headings": ["Steps", "Good to know", "Related topics"], "labels": ["Cancel", "Draft the reference", "Draft the title", "Save", "Translate"], "text": "Help › Open Bible Stories Translate a story Draft the story title, each frame and the reference line, with the picture and the English story beside you. Stories are drafted part by part: the title, each frame, then the reference. Steps Open a story and click Translate . Click Draft the title (1) and type the title. Click Save . For each frame, click \"Draft frame N\" (2), look at the picture and the English text, and type your translation. Click Save (1). At the end, click Draft the reference and translate the line \"A Bible story from: …\". To change a saved part, click its text. To cancel, click Cancel . Good to know The story's percent drafted on the left goes up as you save parts. Notes, words and questions for the frame are in the helps panel on the right. They stay on that frame after you click Save . The Comments tab shows the newest comment of that frame. The frame box is as tall as the picture and text beside it. Longer text scrolls inside the box; drag its corner to make it taller. Stories have no verse numbers or spans. Related topics Understand a story Check a story Find a story on Home"}, {"id": "obs-understand", "url": "obs-understand", "title": "Understand a story", "section": "Open Bible Stories", "status": "shipped", "kind": "feature", "summary": "Read each frame with its picture and the story helps before you translate.", "where": "", "headings": ["Steps", "Good to know", "Related topics"], "labels": ["Notes", "Questions", "Understand", "Words"], "text": "Help › Open Bible Stories Understand a story Read each frame with its picture and the story helps before you translate. It works like reading a passage in a Bible project, frame by frame. Steps Open a story and click Understand . Read the title and each frame with its picture. Click a frame number on the left to jump to it. Read the Notes , Words and Questions on the right. To leave a comment for your team, click the frame's comment box (1), type, then click outside it to save. Good to know Click a frame first so the helps panel shows that frame's helps (1, 2). The panel shows only the frame you clicked, and stays on it after you save, until you click another frame. Story notes are labelled like verses: frame 1's notes say v1 , and the title's say v0 . A saved comment can't be emptied later. If a comment fails to save, edit the box and click outside it again. Related topics Translate a story Translation notes and Translation Academy articles Write a user comment"}, {"id": "rs-checking-language", "url": "rs-checking-language", "title": "Change the checking language", "section": "Resources and offline", "status": "shipped", "kind": "feature", "summary": "Choose which installed gateway language this project checks against.", "where": "", "headings": ["Steps", "Good to know", "Related topics"], "labels": ["Checking language", "Current", "Settings"], "text": "Help › Resources and offline Change the checking language Choose which installed gateway language this project checks against. Checks use the notes and word articles of one gateway language. Steps If the language isn't listed yet, download it first. See Download source texts . On Home, click Settings on the project card. Under Checking language , click the language you want. The current one is marked Current (1). Read the confirmation. It says how many decisions carry over and how many come back as work. Click \"Change to …\" to confirm. The new language is now marked Current . To cancel, click \"Keep …\" in the confirmation. Good to know Completeness includes Translation Words Links ( _twl ) when that language publishes one. Download the package so Links is installed; the app does not fall back to English Links for those languages. A few gateway organisations publish words but no Links repository. For those, checks use the English Links list with that language's word articles. Two Arabic rows can appear: pick the organisation named under the language. After you change language, Translation Words items are rebuilt from the new Links pin. Decisions carry over only when the same check still exists. The language already in use for checking is not offered again as a new choice. When tC4 asks you to confirm a change of checking language, the two actions sit side by side. A decision carries over only when the new notes have the same check on the same words. Notes in another language are often split differently, so few may carry over. That is expected. Nothing is deleted. Decisions that don't carry over stop counting, and those checks come back as To do. A finished book may not be finished any more. The language set must be complete: notes, words and academy. The confirmation names each language as the list does, for example \"Español (Latinoamérica)\". When two packages share a name, such as the two Français rows, each also names its owner. Related topics Download source texts Project settings Decide on a check item (valid, invalid, to do)"}, {"id": "rs-guided-fix", "url": "rs-guided-fix", "title": "Fix a missing resource", "section": "Resources and offline", "status": "shipped", "kind": "feature", "summary": "When a checking tool needs a resource that is not on this computer, tC4 offers three ways to get it.", "where": "", "headings": ["Steps", "Good to know", "Related topics"], "labels": ["Check", "Close", "Fix this…", "Needs downloading", "Re-pin", "Unavailable offline"], "text": "Help › Resources and offline Fix a missing resource When a checking tool needs a resource that is not on this computer, tC4 offers three ways to get it. You usually see this with a project imported or copied from another computer. The tool card shows Needs downloading or Unavailable offline . Steps In Check , click Fix this… on the tool card. Choose one way: 1 · Download the pinned version. Needs the internet. 2 · Use a version that is installed. Click a version, check how many decisions carry over, then click Re-pin . 3 · Sideload it from a file. Drop a Scripture Burrito zip of that exact release. To cancel, click Close or the ✕. Nothing changes. Good to know Offline, use option 2 or 3, or turn on the internet. See Working offline . A tool that isn't ready never blocks the rest of your work. Related topics Working offline Download source texts Import a translationCore 3 project"}, {"id": "rs-offline", "url": "rs-offline", "title": "Working offline", "section": "Resources and offline", "status": "shipped", "kind": "feature", "summary": "tC4 works without the internet. It asks before any step that needs it.", "where": "", "headings": ["Steps", "What needs the internet", "Good to know", "Related topics"], "labels": ["Not now", "Turn on internet", "Use the internet"], "text": "Help › Resources and offline Working offline tC4 works without the internet. It asks before any step that needs it. Everything you need to translate and check with the English helps comes with the app, and your projects stay on your computer. Steps Work as usual. No internet is needed. When a step needs the internet, tC4 asks \"Turn on the internet?\". Click Turn on internet (1), or Not now to stay offline. To turn the internet off again, click the round account button at the top right and switch off Use the internet (1). Closing tC4 also turns it off. What needs the internet Download source texts Update pinned helps and Update scripture texts Downloading a missing resource in Fix a missing resource Signing in and sharing on Door43 Translating, checking, aligning, exporting and importing all work offline. Good to know Nothing from your projects is sent when you turn the internet on for downloads. Keep regular backups of your projects folder. See Where your projects are stored . Related topics Download source texts Update pinned helps Where your projects are stored"}, {"id": "rs-source-texts", "url": "rs-source-texts", "title": "Download source texts", "section": "Resources and offline", "status": "shipped", "kind": "feature", "summary": "Download source texts and helps in another gateway language from Door43.", "where": "", "headings": ["Steps", "Good to know", "Related topics"], "labels": ["Book", "Cancel", "Choose your gateway language", "Close", "Downloading …", "Get source texts", "Manage source texts →", "Package contents", "Read the list", "Settings", "Turn on internet", "⇣ Download package"], "text": "Help › Resources and offline Download source texts Download source texts and helps in another gateway language from Door43. A gateway language is the language your team reads the source and helps in. English comes with the app. Steps On Home, click Settings on the project card, then Manage source texts → . The project opens with Get source texts on top. Under Choose your gateway language , click a language (1). If \"Turn on the internet?\" appears, click Turn on internet . See Working offline . Choose the Book . Check the Package contents . Click a card to leave it out, or again to add it back. Click ⇣ Download package . When it says \"✓ {book} · {language} is ready\", click Close . While the package downloads, the bottom of the dialog shows Downloading … with the repository it is fetching, and a Cancel button. They stay there if you choose another book or go back to the list of languages. Click Cancel to stop the download. To leave without downloading, click Close or the ✕. Closing the dialog doesn't stop a download that has started; open Get source texts again to see it or cancel it. Good to know For most gateway languages the package includes a Translation Words Links repository ( …_twl ) as well as Translation Words. Leave it selected so checking can finish installing. Two rows can share a name, like the two Français rows. The organisation under the name tells them apart. The versions in the list are the newest on Door43, not the ones your project uses. To check in the downloaded language, choose it in Project settings . See Change the checking language . Offline, the dialog says \"tC4 did not read the Door43 list of source texts.\" with Read the list . The line under it, \"This organisation publishes nothing tC4 can use…\", only means the list hasn't been read. If some downloads fail, the dialog lists them. Related topics Change the checking language Working offline What comes with the app"}, {"id": "rs-upgrade-helps", "url": "rs-upgrade-helps", "title": "Update pinned helps", "section": "Resources and offline", "status": "shipped", "kind": "feature", "summary": "See whether newer notes, words and questions are out, and move your project to them when you are ready.", "where": "", "headings": ["Steps", "Good to know", "Related topics"], "labels": ["Check for updates", "Get source texts", "Keep the current release", "Manage source texts →", "Settings", "Turn on internet", "Upgrade", "✓ Up to date"], "text": "Help › Resources and offline Update pinned helps See whether newer notes, words and questions are out, and move your project to them when you are ready. A project keeps the exact versions it started with, so checks stay stable. Updating is always your choice. Steps On Home, click Settings on the project card, then Manage source texts → . The project opens with Get source texts on top. Click Check for updates (1). If \"Turn on the internet?\" appears, click Turn on internet . See Working offline . Each set shows ✓ Up to date or \"Upgrade N resource(s)\" with each change, for example en_tn v86 → v91 . Click \"Upgrade N resource(s)\", read the confirmation, and click Upgrade . To cancel, click Keep the current release . Good to know An upgrade\\'s progress, its confirmation and any failure show only in the project where you clicked Upgrade . If you close and open the project again while Check for updates is still waiting, the button works again. Decisions that match a check in the new release carry over. The rest come back as work. Nothing is deleted. A new project already uses the helps that come with the app, so it says ✓ Up to date . A project that checks in another language lists that language as the primary set and English as the fallback. tC4 doesn't check for updates by itself. Related topics Update scripture texts Change the checking language Working offline"}, {"id": "rs-upgrade-texts", "url": "rs-upgrade-texts", "title": "Update scripture texts", "section": "Resources and offline", "status": "shipped", "kind": "feature", "summary": "Move the project to a newer release of the Greek or Hebrew text, or of the gateway Bible.", "where": "", "headings": ["Steps", "Good to know", "Related topics"], "labels": ["Check for updates", "Get source texts", "Manage source texts →", "Scripture texts", "Settings", "Turn on internet", "Upgrade", "✓ Every scripture text is up to date"], "text": "Help › Resources and offline Update scripture texts Move the project to a newer release of the Greek or Hebrew text, or of the gateway Bible. Steps On Home, click Settings on the project card, then Manage source texts → . The project opens with Get source texts on top. Click Check for updates (1). If \"Turn on the internet?\" appears, click Turn on internet . See Working offline . Look under Scripture texts . Each text with a newer release has its own row. Click Upgrade on a row, read the confirmation, and confirm. If everything is current, it says ✓ Every scripture text is up to date . Good to know An upgrade\\'s progress, its confirmation and any failure show only in the project where you clicked Upgrade . If you close and open the project again while Check for updates is still waiting, the button works again. Gateway Bible: nothing in your work changes. The source pane shows the new text. Greek or Hebrew text: alignments are kept, but the affected verses lose their valid mark so you can check them again. See Mark an alignment done . Only the texts your books need are offered. A New Testament project is never offered the Hebrew text. Save any open alignment edit first. Related topics Update pinned helps Mark an alignment done Working offline"}, {"id": "rs-whats-included", "url": "rs-whats-included", "title": "What comes with the app", "section": "Resources and offline", "status": "shipped", "kind": "feature", "summary": "tC4 comes with English source texts and helps and the Greek and Hebrew texts, so you can translate and check without the internet.", "where": "", "headings": ["See which version a tool uses", "Good to know", "Related topics"], "labels": ["Check"], "text": "Help › Resources and offline What comes with the app tC4 comes with English source texts and helps and the Greek and Hebrew texts, so you can translate and check without the internet. Nothing to do: these are ready when you create a project. Resource What it is Version when this page was written ULT unfoldingWord Literal Text (English) v91 UST unfoldingWord Simplified Text (English) v91 UGNT Greek New Testament v0.34 UHB Hebrew Bible v3.0.0 Translation Notes English notes v91 Translation Words English word articles v91 Translation Words Links English check-list links ( en_twl ) v91 Translation Academy English lessons v91 Translation Questions English questions v91 UGL / UHL Greek and Hebrew lexicons included Open Bible Stories English stories v9 Story notes, word links, questions English story helps v13, v3, v10 Story pictures Pictures for each frame included See which version a tool uses Open a checking tool from Check . Read the line under the filters (1). Good to know Translation Words Links is a separate resource from Translation Words. The app ships both for English so checks use a released list, not an unreleased export. A newer app may come with newer versions. To see the version a project uses, see See which version a tool uses . For another gateway language, see Download source texts . Related topics Download source texts Working offline Update pinned helps"}, {"id": "section-align", "url": "section-align", "title": "Align", "section": "Align", "status": "shipped", "kind": "feature", "summary": "Link each word of your translation to the original language.", "where": "", "headings": [], "labels": [], "text": "Help Align Link each word of your translation to the original language. The parts of the screen are explained in Open the Align tool . Open the Align tool Align links each word of your translation to the original-language word it translates. Open it from Check. Link your words to the source Click a word in the word bank and then the card it belongs under, or drag it there. Align a phrase (merge and split cards) When several source words are translated together, merge their cards into one phrase card. SPLIT undoes it. Align to the gateway text or the original language Align to the gateway words you can read (which already carry the link to the original), or directly to the Greek or Hebrew words. Alignment suggestions tC4 can learn from the verses you have already aligned and suggest links for the next ones. You confirm or reject each one. Mark an alignment done When a verse is aligned, mark it valid. It counts toward the verses-aligned progress."}, {"id": "section-bible-projects", "url": "section-bible-projects", "title": "Bible projects", "section": "Bible projects", "status": "shipped", "kind": "feature", "summary": "Create, import, open and export a Bible translation project.", "where": "", "headings": [], "labels": [], "text": "Help Bible projects Create, import, open and export a Bible translation project. Create a Bible project Start a new Bible translation project: blank, from USFM files, or from a Scripture Burrito. You add books right after. Add a book Add one book, or several at once, to a Bible project. Open a book and move between chapters Click a book tile on Home to open it. Inside the project, the book list on the left moves you between books and chapters. Import a translationCore 3 project Bring translationCore 3 work into tC4 as a new project, from the project zips translationCore 3 exported. Export a book as PDF Make a print-ready PDF of the open book, laid out with your page setup. Export a book as USFM (aligned or plain) Save the open book as a USFM file, with your word alignments or as plain text. Export a project as a Scripture Burrito Save the whole project, with alignments and checking decisions, as one Scripture Burrito zip."}, {"id": "section-check", "url": "section-check", "title": "Check", "section": "Check", "status": "shipped", "kind": "feature", "summary": "Check your draft with Translation Notes and Translation Words, and preview it.", "where": "", "headings": [], "labels": [], "text": "Help Check Check your draft with Translation Notes and Translation Words, and preview it. The parts of the screen are explained in Choose a checking tool . Choose a checking tool Check lists the checking tools for the open book and how far you have got in each one. Check with Translation Notes Go through the hard places in the book, such as figures of speech or abstract nouns, and check how you translated each one. Check with Translation Words Go through every key term in the book, such as God, grace or apostle, and check how you translated each one. Decide on a check item (valid, invalid, to do) For each check, select the words in your translation, then mark the item valid or invalid, or leave it to do. Bookmark a check Flag a check item to come back to it later, for example to discuss with your team. Write a check comment Add a note to a check item about what you decided or what to ask your team. Filter and sort check items Narrow the list to what still needs work, and change the order of items. Community Checking preview Read the whole book as a printed page, set up the page, and export a copy for reviewers."}, {"id": "section-getting-started", "url": "section-getting-started", "title": "Getting started", "section": "Getting started", "status": "shipped", "kind": "feature", "summary": "Install tC4, find your way around Home, and learn the three modes.", "where": "", "headings": [], "labels": [], "text": "Help Getting started Install tC4, find your way around Home, and learn the three modes. The parts of the screen are explained in The Home screen . Install and start tC4 Download translationCore 4 for your computer, install or unpack it, and start it. It opens on the Home screen. The Home screen Home lists your projects. From here you open a book, go back to where you stopped, or add a project. Understand, Translate and Check — the three modes Every open project has three modes. Understand the passage, Translate it, then Check it — in any order. Saving and checkpoints tC4 saves as you work. The indicator at the top right shows whether your last change was saved, and offers Retry if it wasn't. Resume where you left off The banner at the top of Home takes you back to the last place you changed something, in the mode you were using. Switch project or go back Home Click the project name at the top left to leave the project and go back to Home, where you can open another project or book."}, {"id": "section-obs", "url": "section-obs", "title": "Open Bible Stories", "section": "Open Bible Stories", "status": "shipped", "kind": "feature", "summary": "Translate the fifty Open Bible Stories frame by frame.", "where": "", "headings": [], "labels": [], "text": "Help Open Bible Stories Translate the fifty Open Bible Stories frame by frame. Start an Open Bible Stories project Create a project for translating all fifty Open Bible Stories into your language. Find a story on Home The stories card shows three stories. Show all 50 to pick any story. Translate a story Draft the story title, each frame and the reference line, with the picture and the English story beside you. Understand a story Read each frame with its picture and the story helps before you translate. Check a story Check a story with Translation Notes and Translation Words, frame by frame. Story preview and export Preview a story as a page with its pictures, choose the layout, and export it for reviewers. Export stories as PDF or Markdown Save a printable PDF of your stories, a Story Markdown zip, or a Scripture Burrito."}, {"id": "section-resources", "url": "section-resources", "title": "Resources and offline", "section": "Resources and offline", "status": "shipped", "kind": "feature", "summary": "Source texts, helps, downloads, upgrades and working without a network.", "where": "", "headings": [], "labels": [], "text": "Help Resources and offline Source texts, helps, downloads, upgrades and working without a network. What comes with the app tC4 comes with English source texts and helps and the Greek and Hebrew texts, so you can translate and check without the internet. Download source texts Download source texts and helps in another gateway language from Door43. Change the checking language Choose which installed gateway language this project checks against. Update pinned helps See whether newer notes, words and questions are out, and move your project to them when you are ready. Update scripture texts Move the project to a newer release of the Greek or Hebrew text, or of the gateway Bible. Fix a missing resource When a checking tool needs a resource that is not on this computer, tC4 offers three ways to get it. Working offline tC4 works without the internet. It asks before any step that needs it."}, {"id": "section-settings", "url": "section-settings", "title": "Settings", "section": "Settings", "status": "shipped", "kind": "feature", "summary": "Project settings, the app language, and where tC4 keeps your work.", "where": "", "headings": [], "labels": [], "text": "Help Settings Project settings, the app language, and where tC4 keeps your work. Project settings Change a project’s text direction, script font or checking language, or manage its source texts. Where your projects are stored tC4 keeps all your projects in one folder in your home folder. Back it up regularly. Change the app language Show translationCore's menus, buttons and dialogs in English, Spanish (Latin America), French, Hindi, and five more languages shipped after 522."}, {"id": "section-translate", "url": "section-translate", "title": "Translate", "section": "Translate", "status": "shipped", "kind": "feature", "summary": "Draft your translation beside the source texts, a section or a verse at a time.", "where": "", "headings": [], "labels": [], "text": "Help Translate Draft your translation beside the source texts, a section or a verse at a time. The parts of the screen are explained in The Translate screen . The Translate screen Translate is where you draft. The source text is on the left, your translation is beside it, and the helps are on the right. Source tabs — gateway texts and the original language Switch the source column between the literal text, the simplified text and the Greek or Hebrew original. Draft a section Type a whole section as one piece of text, then place the verse numbers. This is the main way to draft in tC4. Place verse numbers After typing a section, pick up each verse number and click the word where that verse begins. Make or break a verse span Join two or more verses into one span, such as 2-3, when your language needs to reorder their content. Break the span again the same way. Bible projects only. Draft or revise one verse Switch to Verse to draft a single verse, or to fix one verse without opening the whole section. Paragraphs and poetry Start a new paragraph with Enter and indent a poetry line with Tab while you type a section. The helps panel in Translate While you draft, the helps panel shows the notes, word links, questions, simplified text and your own comments for the verses in focus. Editing a verse you already checked or aligned When you change a verse that has check decisions or alignments, tC4 flags them for review. Nothing is deleted."}, {"id": "section-troubleshooting", "url": "section-troubleshooting", "title": "Troubleshooting and known limits", "section": "Troubleshooting and known limits", "status": "shipped", "kind": "feature", "summary": "What the messages mean, and what this version of tC4 doesn’t do.", "where": "", "headings": [], "labels": [], "text": "Help Troubleshooting and known limits What the messages mean, and what this version of tC4 doesn’t do. When a save fails If a save fails, your text stays on screen. Click Retry, and don’t close tC4 until it says Saved. Messages that protect your project When tC4 finds something unexpected, it stops rather than risk your work, and tells you what to do. If the app closed unexpectedly or the window went blank If tC4 closed unexpectedly or the window went blank, it recovers your projects. Window › Reload brings it back without quitting. Windows, macOS and Linux notes What to expect when you install and start tC4 on Windows, macOS and Linux. Share to Door43 Put a project on Door43 so others can read it. You sign in once and review where it goes before anything is uploaded. Working as a team on several computers Share a Bible project on Door43, then each teammate opens it from Door43 and uses Sync with Door43 to swap work. Features tC4 doesn’t have Things people often ask for that this version of tC4 doesn’t do."}, {"id": "section-understand", "url": "section-understand", "title": "Understand", "section": "Understand", "status": "shipped", "kind": "feature", "summary": "Read a passage with its notes, word links and questions before you translate.", "where": "", "headings": [], "labels": [], "text": "Help Understand Read a passage with its notes, word links and questions before you translate. The parts of the screen are explained in Read a passage before you translate . Read a passage before you translate Understand shows the source passage section by section, with its helps beside it and a box for your own comments. Translation notes and Translation Academy articles Translation notes explain hard phrases verse by verse. Each note can link to a Translation Academy article that teaches the translation issue behind it. Word links (Translation Words) Word links point out the key biblical terms in the passage and link each to a Translation Words article that defines it. Translation questions Translation questions ask what the passage says. Use them to test whether your translation communicates the meaning. The simplified text tab The helps panel can show the meaning-based simplified text next to what you are reading. Write a user comment Write down what a passage means, who is speaking, and what will be hard to translate. Your comments are saved with the project and shown in Translate."}, {"id": "section-whats-new", "url": "section-whats-new", "title": "Release notes", "section": "Release notes", "status": "shipped", "kind": "feature", "summary": "What each release of tC4 added, oldest first. The newest is at the bottom.", "where": "", "headings": [], "labels": [], "text": "Help Release notes What each release of tC4 added, oldest first. The newest is at the bottom. alpha.1 — First public build The first public snapshot of translationCore 4, a ground-up rewrite. Create a project and draft beside source texts. alpha.2 — Check and align Check a whole book with Translation Notes and Translation Words, and align verses to the original language. alpha.4 — Open and resume Resume where you left off after a restart, checkpoints on mode changes, and the new design for Understand, Translate and Check. alpha.5 — Drafting for real Draft a whole section at a time, place verse numbers, join verses into spans, format paragraphs and poetry, and read a passage with helps. alpha.6 — Suggestions and resources Alignment suggestions, check comments and bookmarks, resource upgrades, and a guided fix for missing resources. alpha.7 — Open Bible Stories Create an Open Bible Stories project, translate stories frame by frame with pictures, read them with helps, and check them. Sep 27, 2026 — one + Add a project button on Home Home now has one + Add a project button instead of three. It opens a dialog where you choose a new Bible, new Open Bible Stories, or an import. The Home heading is now Your projects. What's new in 4.0 rc.1 Get your work out and bring existing work in: export a book as PDF, USFM or a Scripture Burrito, export stories as Markdown, PDF or a burrito, and import translationCore 3, USFM and Scripture Burrito files. Plus project Settings on the Home card, new books opening in Understand, English helps v91 in the box, and Spacing and Paper size for printing. What's new in 4.0 rc.2 The public beta. A preparing window on first start, numbered repeated words and multi-word placing in Align, drag-select in Check, and Previous and Next beside the marking buttons. Updated October 20: poetry verses open whole, and alignments follow their words. What's new in 4.1 Helps cards grouped by verse with named Translation Academy links and word intros, Clear alignments, story frame conflicts on sync, project-format sync guards, and app language that follows the computer until you choose."}, {"id": "st-app-language", "url": "st-app-language", "title": "Change the app language", "section": "Settings", "status": "shipped", "kind": "feature", "summary": "Choose the language of the buttons, menus and messages in tC4. Until you choose one, tC4 follows the language of this computer; after you pick, it keeps that choice.", "where": "", "headings": ["Steps", "What changes and what doesn't", "Languages in the list", "Find English again", "Related topics"], "labels": ["Aplicar", "App language", "Apply", "Cancel", "Check", "Language", "Translate", "Understand"], "text": "Help › Settings Change the app language Choose the language of the buttons, menus and messages in tC4. Until you choose one, tC4 follows the language of this computer; after you pick, it keeps that choice. Your translation and your projects do not change. Steps Click the round person icon at the top right (1), then App language (2). The menu is the same on Home and inside a project. Click the Language box and choose a language. The whole app switches to that language right away, so you can see what it looks like before you decide. Click Apply to keep it. In Spanish the button reads Aplicar . To go back to the language you had, click Cancel or ✕ instead. translationCore remembers your choice the next time you open it. What changes and what doesn't Changes: menus, buttons, labels and dialogs, such as Understand , Translate and Check at the top of a project. Doesn't change: your translation, the source texts (such as the ULT) and the translation helps (notes, words and questions). These stay in the language they were written in. To choose the language of the checking helps, see Checking language . Languages in the list English, Español (Latinoamérica), Français, हिन्दी, Português (Brasil), Bahasa Indonesia, Українська, Русский and Tiếng Việt. The first four came with #522 ; the next five with #622 . Find English again If you can't read the current language, the person icon is always at the top right, and App language is in the account menu (after Help and guides). English is always first in the list. Related topics The Home screen Project settings Change the checking language"}, {"id": "st-project-settings", "url": "st-project-settings", "title": "Project settings", "section": "Settings", "status": "partial", "kind": "feature", "summary": "Change a project’s text direction, script font or checking language, or manage its source texts.", "where": "", "headings": ["Steps", "Good to know", "Related topics"], "labels": ["Cancel", "Checking language", "Left to right", "Manage source texts →", "Right to left", "Save changes", "Script font", "Settings", "Text direction"], "text": "Help › Settings Project settings Change a project’s text direction, script font or checking language, or manage its source texts. Steps On Home, click Settings on the project card. Change what you need: Text direction : Left to right or Right to left . Checking language (1). See Change the checking language . Manage source texts → (2). See Download source texts . Script font (3): the font your translation is shown in. Click Save changes . To cancel, click Cancel or the ✕, or press Esc. Nothing is saved. Good to know The settings are only on the Home card, not in the top bar. To get back to Home, click the project name at the top left. The fonts are Noto Sans (default), Charis SIL, Scheherazade New (Arabic script), Awami Nastaliq (Nastaliq) and Padauk (Myanmar). Pick the one that shows your letters correctly. The name and language can't be changed in this version. Project settings show them greyed out, with the note \"Renaming arrives with a later increment.\" Related topics Change the checking language Download source texts Where your projects are stored"}, {"id": "st-where-projects-live", "url": "st-where-projects-live", "title": "Where your projects are stored", "section": "Settings", "status": "shipped", "kind": "feature", "summary": "tC4 keeps all your projects in one folder in your home folder. Back it up regularly.", "where": "", "headings": ["Back up your projects", "Good to know", "Related topics"], "labels": ["+ Add a project", "Receive work from a file", "Save or receive work by USB"], "text": "Help › Settings Where your projects are stored tC4 keeps all your projects in one folder in your home folder. Back it up regularly. \"Only on this computer\" (1) on a project card means the project is stored only in this folder. System Folder Windows C:\\Users\\<you>\\pankosmia\\tc4-projects macOS ~/pankosmia/tc4-projects Linux ~/pankosmia/tc4-projects Back up your projects Close tC4. Copy the whole tc4-projects folder to a USB drive or another backup place. Good to know Uninstalling tC4 keeps your projects. To move one project to another computer, use Save or receive work by USB , or export a Scripture Burrito and open it there with + Add a project › Receive work from a file . That's safer than copying folders. Don't change files in the folder while tC4 is open. tC4 notices and stops to protect the project. See Messages that protect your project . Related topics Export a project as a Scripture Burrito Import a translationCore 3 project Messages that protect your project"}, {"id": "tr-draft-section", "url": "tr-draft-section", "title": "Draft a section", "section": "Translate", "status": "shipped", "kind": "feature", "summary": "Type a whole section as one piece of text, then place the verse numbers. This is the main way to draft in tC4.", "where": "", "headings": ["Steps", "Good to know", "Related topics"], "labels": ["Cancel", "Draft section {span}", "Place verse numbers", "Save section", "Section", "To place", "Translate", "Type", "Verse"], "text": "Help › Translate Draft a section Type a whole section as one piece of text, then place the verse numbers. This is the main way to draft in tC4. You type the section straight through, the way you would say it, and add the verse numbers afterwards. Steps In Translate , make sure the switch above your translation says Section . On the row you want, click the Draft section {span} button at its top right (1). It is a small rounded button, always visible. The editor opens in Type mode. Type your translation of the whole section. Click Place verse numbers and put each number where its verse begins. See Place verse numbers . Click Save section . To close the editor without saving, click Cancel . Good to know The editor is as tall as the source passage beside it. Longer text scrolls inside the box. To make the box taller, drag its corner. To change a saved section, click its Draft section {span} label again. Clicking the saved text does not open the editor. You can type the verse numbers yourself: start a line with the number, for example 5 no nos salvó… . The first verse number of a section is always at the start. Wait a moment after the editor opens before you type, and check the start of your text before you save. While an editor is open, the Section / Verse switch can't be used. A verse number you leave in the To place box is saved as an empty verse. Related topics Place verse numbers Paragraphs and poetry Draft or revise one verse The Translate screen"}, {"id": "tr-edit-after-check", "url": "tr-edit-after-check", "title": "Editing a verse you already checked or aligned", "section": "Translate", "status": "shipped", "kind": "feature", "summary": "When you change a verse that has check decisions or alignments, tC4 flags them for review. Nothing is deleted.", "where": "", "headings": ["Steps", "Good to know", "Related topics"], "labels": ["Align", "Check", "Invalid", "Translate", "✓ Mark valid"], "text": "Help › Translate Editing a verse you already checked or aligned When you change a verse that has check decisions or alignments, tC4 flags them for review. Nothing is deleted. Checks and alignments are tied to the exact words of a verse, so when those words change tC4 marks what may no longer fit. Steps Change a verse in Translate that you already checked or aligned. Go to Check and open the checking tool. A notice at the top says how many decisions need review. Click the Invalid filter to see those items, and decide each one again. Open Align for the verse. Words you changed are back in the word bank. Place them and click ✓ Mark valid again. Good to know A check decision whose words changed becomes Invalid . It is kept, not deleted. In Align, links to the words you didn't change are kept. The verse shows as to do again. A link stays on the word it was placed on, even if you add or remove words before it. If you delete a linked word, its link goes too. If you change a word that comes more than once, and tC4 can't tell which copy you kept, the verse is flagged for review. Open it in Align, check the links, and click ✓ Mark valid again. A verse that needs review shows a notice at the top of the verse in Align and is marked in the verse list. Making or breaking a verse span has the same effect on those verses. A checking tool may reopen at the item you were on. Use the Invalid filter to find every item that needs review. Related topics Decide on a check item (valid, invalid, to do) Filter and sort check items Link your words to the source Make or break a verse span"}, {"id": "tr-formatting", "url": "tr-formatting", "title": "Paragraphs and poetry", "section": "Translate", "status": "shipped", "kind": "feature", "summary": "Start a new paragraph with Enter and indent a poetry line with Tab while you type a section.", "where": "", "headings": ["Steps", "Poetry you already have", "Good to know", "Related topics"], "labels": ["Draft section {span}", "Save section", "Type"], "text": "Help › Translate Paragraphs and poetry Start a new paragraph with Enter and indent a poetry line with Tab while you type a section. This works in the section editor's Type mode. Steps Open a section with Draft section {span} . Type your text. Press Enter where a new paragraph begins. At the start of a poetry line, press Tab . Press Tab again for a second level, or Shift+Tab to take an indent back. Place the verse numbers and click Save section . Poetry you already have When you open a verse of poetry, the editor shows the whole verse, every line of the poem. Each line starts with its indent: one tab for the first level, two for the second, three for the third. A paragraph break or chunk break inside the verse shows as a blank line, and a heading inside a verse shows as its own line. Keep those lines as they are, and change only the words. Good to know tC4 has three poetry levels. A fourth tab is saved as the third level. In the Verse editor, Tab and Enter close the editor and save. To start a new poetry line there, press Shift+Enter . To indent a line, use the section editor. The source text shows its own paragraphs and poetry lines, so you can follow them. Headings, footnotes and other formatting can't be added in the editor yet. Related topics Draft a section Place verse numbers"}, {"id": "tr-helps-panel", "url": "tr-helps-panel", "title": "The helps panel in Translate", "section": "Translate", "status": "shipped", "kind": "feature", "summary": "While you draft, the helps panel shows the notes, word links, questions, simplified text and your own comments for the verses in focus. Notes are grouped under section and verse headings, and word cards open with the first paragraph of their article.", "where": "", "headings": ["Steps", "The tabs", "Good to know", "Related topics"], "labels": ["Abstract Nouns →", "Comments", "GST", "INTRODUCTION", "Notes", "Questions", "Read the article →", "Save", "Section", "Show less", "Show more", "Toggle helps panel", "Translate", "UST", "Understand", "VERSE 1", "Verse", "Words"], "text": "Help › Translate The helps panel in Translate While you draft, the helps panel shows the notes, word links, questions, simplified text and your own comments for the verses in focus. Notes are grouped under section and verse headings, and word cards open with the first paragraph of their article. It sits on the right of Translate , next to your draft. Steps If the panel is hidden, click Toggle helps panel at the top right of the chapter. Click a tab. Open a section or verse editor. The panel narrows to the verses you are editing. The tabs Notes : translation notes, grouped under uppercase headings such as INTRODUCTION and VERSE 1 . Each note that has a Translation Academy article links by the article's real name (for example Abstract Nouns → ), not a generic label. Words : key terms. Each card shows the first paragraph of the Translation Words article and Read the article → . Click a card to highlight every occurrence of that word in the section. Questions : questions with answers, to test your draft's meaning. UST : the simplified text of the chapter. Some gateway languages give this tab another name, such as GST . Comments : comments you wrote in Understand . It says \"Nothing in this chapter.\" when there are none. Good to know The helps follow what you clicked: a section in Section mode, a verse in Verse mode, or a story frame. They stay on it after you click Save , until you click another one or leave the chapter. Point at a Notes card to highlight its phrase in the source; click it to keep the highlight. Click a Words card to highlight every matching word across the section you are reading. Long notes have Show more and Show less . Drag the vertical divider between your draft and the helps to make the panel wider or narrower. Toggle helps panel shows or hides the panel completely (one button; there is no separate widen control). Helps articles open with a floating close control above a divider, and render line breaks, superscripts and underlines from the source cleanly. Each help is explained in the Understand section. Related topics The Translate screen Translation notes and Translation Academy articles Word links (Translation Words) Translation questions Write a user comment"}, {"id": "tr-place-verse-numbers", "url": "tr-place-verse-numbers", "title": "Place verse numbers", "section": "Translate", "status": "shipped", "kind": "feature", "summary": "After typing a section, click each verse number, then click the word where that verse begins.", "where": "", "headings": ["Steps", "Good to know", "Related topics"], "labels": ["Cancel", "Place verse numbers", "Save section", "To place", "Type"], "text": "Help › Translate Place verse numbers After typing a section, click each verse number, then click the word where that verse begins. Each verse number is a small orange marker, and numbers you haven't placed yet wait in the To place box. Steps In the section editor, click Place verse numbers . Click a number in the To place box (1). The box says \"Click a verse number, then click the word that begins the verse.\" The box now says \"Click the word where verse 10 begins\". Click that word in the text. Repeat for every number, then click Save section . To go back to typing, click Type . The numbers you placed are kept. Cancel closes the editor without saving. Good to know To move a number that is already placed, click it in the text, then click another word. If a number lands on a nearby word as soon as you click it, click it again and click the right word. When you click a placed number, the box still says \"Every verse is placed. Click a verse number to move where its verse begins.\" Follow the line under it: \"Click the word where verse 10 begins\". A number can't pass another number, and the first number of the section can't move. Putting a number on the word where another verse begins joins them into a span. See Make or break a verse span . Related topics Draft a section Make or break a verse span Paragraphs and poetry"}, {"id": "tr-screen", "url": "tr-screen", "title": "The Translate screen", "section": "Translate", "status": "shipped", "kind": "feature", "summary": "Translate is where you draft. The source text is on the left, your translation is beside it, and the helps are on the right.", "where": "", "headings": ["Draft a section", "Make room", "The helps panel", "Related topics"], "labels": ["Cancel", "Comments", "Draft section", "Greek", "Hebrew", "Notes", "Place verse numbers", "Questions", "Save section", "Section", "Toggle helps panel", "Translate", "ULT", "UST", "Verse", "Words"], "text": "Help › Translate The Translate screen Translate is where you draft. The source text is on the left, your translation is beside it, and the helps are on the right. Each row puts a section of the source text next to your translation of it. Draft a section Open a book, then click Translate at the top. Pick a chapter in the book list on the left. Choose the source text with the tabs above it: ULT (literal), UST (simplified) or the original language ( Greek or Hebrew ). The line under the tabs names the text and its version. Choose Section to draft a whole section at once, or Verse to draft one verse at a time. Click Draft section beside the section you want. Type the text, then use Place verse numbers . See Draft a section . Click Save section . To leave the section as it was, click Cancel . Make room The button at the top left of the page shows or hides the book list. At the top right of the page, Toggle helps panel shows or hides the helps. Drag the vertical divider to change the panel width. The helps panel The panel shows help for the section you are working on: Notes , Words , Questions , the simplified text ( UST ) and Comments from Understand. Click a help card and the words it is about are highlighted in the source text. See The helps panel in Translate . Translate remembers your choice of Section or Verse for each project. Related topics Draft a section Draft or revise one verse Place verse numbers The helps panel in Translate Source tabs — gateway texts and the original language Understand, Translate and Check — the three modes"}, {"id": "tr-source-texts", "url": "tr-source-texts", "title": "Source tabs — gateway texts and the original language", "section": "Translate", "status": "shipped", "kind": "feature", "summary": "Switch the source column between the literal text, the simplified text and the Greek or Hebrew original.", "where": "", "headings": ["Steps", "Good to know", "Related topics"], "labels": ["GLT", "GST", "Greek", "Hebrew", "Translate", "ULT", "UST", "Understand"], "text": "Help › Translate Source tabs — gateway texts and the original language Switch the source column between the literal text, the simplified text and the Greek or Hebrew original. Your translation stays the same when you switch; only the source column changes. Steps In Translate , look at the tabs above the source column. Click ULT (the literal text), UST (the simplified text) or Greek . An Old Testament book has Hebrew instead of Greek. The caption under the tabs names the text and its version. Good to know The sections follow the selected source. In Titus 1, ULT and UST start with 1–3, while Greek shows the whole chapter as 1–16. Words in curly braces, such as {and}, are part of the source text. They mark words added to make the meaning clear. Some gateway languages name the first two tabs differently, for example GLT and GST . The same tabs are in Understand . If a text is missing, the column says \"This source is not available for this book.\" See Browse and download source texts . Related topics The Translate screen Download source texts Read a passage before you translate"}, {"id": "tr-verse-by-verse", "url": "tr-verse-by-verse", "title": "Draft or revise one verse", "section": "Translate", "status": "shipped", "kind": "feature", "summary": "Switch to Verse to draft a single verse, or to fix one verse without opening the whole section.", "where": "", "headings": ["Steps", "Good to know", "Related topics"], "labels": ["Cancel", "Draft verse {n}", "Save verse", "Section", "Translate", "Verse"], "text": "Help › Translate Draft or revise one verse Switch to Verse to draft a single verse, or to fix one verse without opening the whole section. Verse view is handy for small corrections and for translators who prefer to work one verse at a time. Steps In Translate , click Verse in the switch above your translation. For an empty verse, click its dashed Draft verse {n} button. For a drafted verse, click its text. Type in the editor. Click Save verse , or click outside the editor. To throw away your change, click Cancel instead. Good to know The helps panel follows the verse you are editing. To make a verse span, use Section view. See Make or break a verse span . A poetry verse opens with all its lines. Shift+Enter starts a new line; Tab and Enter save and close the editor. See Paragraphs and poetry . Related topics Draft a section The Translate screen Saving and checkpoints"}, {"id": "tr-verse-spans", "url": "tr-verse-spans", "title": "Make or break a verse span", "section": "Translate", "status": "shipped", "kind": "feature", "summary": "Join two or more verses into one span, such as 2-3, when your language needs to reorder their content. Break the span again the same way. Bible projects only.", "where": "", "headings": ["Steps", "Break a span", "Good to know", "Related topics"], "labels": ["Draft section {span}", "Place verse numbers", "Save section", "Type", "Verse"], "text": "Help › Translate Make or break a verse span Join two or more verses into one span, such as 2-3, when your language needs to reorder their content. Break the span again the same way. Bible projects only. A verse span (also called a verse bridge) is two or more verses translated as one unit, for example 2-3 . Steps Open the section with its Draft section {span} label, type the text, and click Place verse numbers . Click the later number in the text, for example 10. Click the word where the earlier verse (9) begins. The tooltip says \"Join verse 10 to verse 9 at …\". Click Save section . The verses are saved as one span, numbered 2-3. Break a span Open the section again, click Place verse numbers , click the 10 and click the word where verse 10 should begin. Then click Save section . Good to know In Type mode you can also start a line with the span, for example 2-3 … . Making or breaking a span marks the checks and alignments of those verses for review. Nothing is deleted. See Editing a verse you already checked or aligned . Spans can't be made in Verse view. Open Bible Stories have frames, not verse numbers, so they have no spans. Related topics Place verse numbers Editing a verse you already checked or aligned Draft a section"}, {"id": "ts-crash-recovery", "url": "ts-crash-recovery", "title": "If the app closed unexpectedly or the window went blank", "section": "Troubleshooting and known limits", "status": "shipped", "kind": "feature", "summary": "If tC4 closed unexpectedly or the window went blank, it recovers your projects. Window › Reload brings it back without quitting.", "where": "", "headings": ["If the window goes blank or stops responding", "If tC4 closed", "Good to know", "Related topics"], "labels": [], "text": "Help › Troubleshooting and known limits If the app closed unexpectedly or the window went blank If tC4 closed unexpectedly or the window went blank, it recovers your projects. Window › Reload brings it back without quitting. tC4 records each step that changes a project. If it stops in the middle of one, it finishes or cleans up at the next start, so no broken project is left. If the window goes blank or stops responding Open the Window menu and click Reload , or press Ctrl+R. tC4 comes back on Home. Open your project again. If you see \"This screen could not be shown.\", click Back to Home , then open the project again. If tC4 closed Start tC4 again. Check that your projects are on Home. If an import was interrupted, import the file again. Good to know Reload takes you to Home. Your projects are still there. Toggle Developer Tools is for developers. You don't need it. Related topics When a save fails Messages that protect your project Where your projects are stored"}, {"id": "ts-not-yet", "url": "ts-not-yet", "title": "Features tC4 doesn’t have", "section": "Troubleshooting and known limits", "status": "not-yet", "kind": "feature", "summary": "Things people often ask for that this version of tC4 doesn’t do.", "where": "", "headings": ["Related topics"], "labels": ["+ Add a project", "Add books from USFM files", "Check for updates", "Open a project from Door43", "Sync with Door43"], "text": "Help › Troubleshooting and known limits Features tC4 doesn’t have Things people often ask for that this version of tC4 doesn’t do. Feature In this version Rename a project or change its language Not available. Project settings show the name and language greyed out. Renumber verses Not available Book introductions Not available Footnotes in the PDF Not available. The switch is shown but can't be turned on. Checking progress on Home Not available. Home shows drafting progress only. Version history, or undo after you close tC4 Not available Export checks as a spreadsheet Not available Automatic update checks Not available. Use Check for updates . See Update pinned helps . Import into an existing project USFM books only, with Add a book › Add books from USFM files . Other imports make a new project. Open a project from Door43, team sync Works: + Add a project › Open a project from Door43 , then Sync with Door43 . See Working as a team on several computers . Right-to-left and complex scripts Work Related topics Working as a team on several computers Share to Door43 Import a translationCore 3 project"}, {"id": "ts-platform", "url": "ts-platform", "title": "Windows, macOS and Linux notes", "section": "Troubleshooting and known limits", "status": "partial", "kind": "feature", "summary": "What to expect when you install and start tC4 on Windows, macOS and Linux.", "where": "", "headings": ["Steps", "Good to know", "Related topics"], "labels": [], "text": "Help › Troubleshooting and known limits Windows, macOS and Linux notes What to expect when you install and start tC4 on Windows, macOS and Linux. Test builds aren't signed yet, so your system may warn you the first time. System What to expect Windows SmartScreen may warn: click More info › Run anyway . You may need the Microsoft Visual C++ runtime. The plain USFM export may not download. macOS The first time, go to System Settings › Privacy & Security and click Open Anyway . Quit tC4 before you install another version. Linux Unzip and run start-tc4.sh . Steps Follow Install and start tC4 . If your system blocks it, follow its \"open anyway\" steps above. Good to know Run only one copy of tC4 at a time. Related topics Install and start tC4 If the app closed unexpectedly or the window went blank"}, {"id": "ts-protect-messages", "url": "ts-protect-messages", "title": "Messages that protect your project", "section": "Troubleshooting and known limits", "status": "shipped", "kind": "feature", "summary": "When tC4 finds something unexpected, it stops rather than risk your work, and tells you what to do.", "where": "", "headings": ["Steps", "Messages when you import", "Messages when you open, save or export", "Good to know", "Related topics"], "labels": ["Add a book", "Add books from USFM files", "Cancel", "From USFM files", "New Bible", "Pictures", "← Choose other files"], "text": "Help › Troubleshooting and known limits Messages that protect your project When tC4 finds something unexpected, it stops rather than risk your work, and tells you what to do. Each message says whether your work is saved, or that nothing was created. Steps Read the message and follow its advice. For a refused import file, click ← Choose other files to pick a good file, or Cancel . If it says \"Ask for help\", contact your support person and leave the project folder as it is. Messages when you import Code Example What to do import.damaged.truncated \"… is not a complete zip file.\" The zip was cut off. Export or copy it again. import.damaged.no-manifest \"… has no manifest.json, so it is not a translationCore 3 project.\" Choose the zip translationCore 3 exported. import.damaged.corrupt-entry \"… is damaged … so it cannot be read.\" Export it from translationCore 3 again. import.name-exists \"A project with this name is already on this computer.\" Type another Bible name. import.write-failed \"The partly written Bible was removed…\" Try again. If it happens again, ask for help. USFM files are checked before anything is made, in New Bible › From USFM files and in Add a book › Add books from USFM files . A file that can't be used gets an orange dot and says why and on which line, for example \"GEN-draft.usfm cannot be added: line 4: chapter 1 appears twice.\" Remove it with its ✕, fix the file and add it again. Messages when you open, save or export Message starts with What to do \"A file changed outside the app.\" Open the project again. tC4 repairs what it can and names what it can't. \"A story file was changed outside the app.\" Undo the outside change, then open the project again. \"This computer's change folder holds another computer's changes.\" A project folder was copied by hand. Ask for help. \"… has no drafted verse yet, so the PDF has nothing to print.\" Draft at least one verse, then export again. \"The app could not print the PDF…\" Close tC4, open it again and export again. For stories, try with Pictures off. \"This project was synced, so the app must open it again.\" A sync changed the project while it was open. Click Home, open the project again, and make your last change again. Home closes in one click even if that save didn't finish. \"The app stopped to protect your project.\" Ask for help. \"A pre-release version of translationCore pinned the Translation Words links…\" Create the project again in this version of tC4. The old Links pin cannot be used. Good to know Write down the code, or take a picture of the message, when you ask for help. Under the red box the checks can still look green. Trust the red box: the file can't be imported. Related topics Import a translationCore 3 project When a save fails Where your projects are stored"}, {"id": "ts-save-problems", "url": "ts-save-problems", "title": "When a save fails", "section": "Troubleshooting and known limits", "status": "shipped", "kind": "feature", "summary": "If a save fails, your text stays on screen. Click Retry, and don’t close tC4 until it says Saved.", "where": "", "headings": ["Steps", "Good to know", "Related topics"], "labels": ["Retry", "Save failed — retry", "Saved", "Saving…", "Unsaved changes", "← All checking tools"], "text": "Help › Troubleshooting and known limits When a save fails If a save fails, your text stays on screen. Click Retry, and don’t close tC4 until it says Saved. The indicator at the top right shows Saved , Saving… , Unsaved changes or Save failed — retry . Steps If you see Save failed — retry , click Retry once and wait. If it fails again, stay on the screen. Moving to another mode may be refused until the save works. If you try to close tC4 with unsaved changes, it warns you. Stay, and try Retry again. If it keeps failing, write down the message and ask for help. Good to know Clicking Retry many times doesn't help. It tries once at a time. In an imported project, writing the first text into an empty verse can fail to save every time. Retry won't fix it. Copy your text somewhere safe before you close tC4, because the verse comes back empty. Cancelling such a verse after typing can show the same message. ← All checking tools still works while a save problem is showing. If the message under the save indicator says This project was synced, so the app must open it again , click Home, open the project again, and make your last change again. Retry won't help in that case. See Messages that protect your project . Related topics Saving and checkpoints If the app closed unexpectedly or the window went blank Messages that protect your project"}, {"id": "ts-share-door43", "url": "ts-share-door43", "title": "Share to Door43", "section": "Troubleshooting and known limits", "status": "shipped", "kind": "feature", "summary": "Put a project on Door43 so others can read it. You sign in once and review where it goes before anything is uploaded.", "where": "", "headings": ["Steps", "Good to know", "Related topics"], "labels": ["+ Add a project", "Not now", "Open a project from Door43", "Share on Door43", "Sign in to Door43", "Sync with Door43", "Turn on internet"], "text": "Help › Troubleshooting and known limits Share to Door43 Put a project on Door43 so others can read it. You sign in once and review where it goes before anything is uploaded. Sharing sends the project to Door43. Later shares upload your changes again. Bible and Open Bible Stories projects share the same way. Steps On Home, click Share on Door43 on the project card (1). tC4 asks \"Turn on the internet?\". Click Turn on internet (1). If you aren't signed in, sign in with your Door43 username or email and password. Choose your account or an organization, review the books, and upload. To cancel, click Not now or the ✕. Nothing is sent. Good to know You can also sign in from the round account button at the top right › Sign in to Door43 . Tick Stay signed in on this computer if you don't want to sign in every time. Each change you share is signed with this computer's account name, which Door43 shows as the author. After the first share, the button on the card is Sync with Door43 : it sends your changes and brings in your teammates'. Teammates get the project with + Add a project › Open a project from Door43 . See Working as a team on several computers . Related topics Export a project as a Scripture Burrito Working offline Working as a team on several computers"}, {"id": "ts-team-sync", "url": "ts-team-sync", "title": "Working as a team on several computers", "section": "Troubleshooting and known limits", "status": "shipped", "kind": "feature", "summary": "Share a project on Door43, then each teammate opens it from Door43 and uses Sync with Door43 to swap work.", "where": "", "headings": ["Start sharing (project owner)", "Join a project (teammate)", "Sync", "Versions the project uses", "Story frame conflicts", "Project format and repaired files", "Good to know", "Related topics"], "labels": ["1 conflict · Review", "1 frame conflict to review", "1.4", "2", "A project file was deleted or damaged on Door43, so tC4 put your copy back.", "Ask for help", "Cancel", "Cancel download", "Download", "Not now", "Open it", "Open project", "Review", "Share on Door43", "This project cannot be opened.", "Turn on internet"], "text": "Help › Troubleshooting and known limits Working as a team on several computers Share a project on Door43, then each teammate opens it from Door43 and uses Sync with Door43 to swap work. Once a Bible or Open Bible Stories project is on Door43, teammates can each work on their own computer and swap changes with Sync with Door43 . Start sharing (project owner) On the Home screen, select Share on Door43 on the project card and follow the steps. See Share to Door43 . On Door43, give each teammate write access to the repository. Teammates can't sync without it. The card now says On Door43 and Shared at the repository, and its button changes to Sync with Door43 . Join a project (teammate) Select + Add a project , then Open a project from Door43 . Type owner/repository , or paste the link to the repository, and click Open project . A private repository opens after you sign in to Door43. If the internet is off, tC4 asks first. Click Turn on internet ; Not now gets nothing. The project keeps the name the team gave it; tC4 doesn't ask for one. tC4 checks every file before it adds the project. A project uses exact versions of its source texts and helps. If some of them are not on your computer, tC4 downloads them first, and the dialog names each one, for example Downloading en_tn v91, which this project uses. Versions left after this one: 8. A bar under it shows how far that version has come. Click Cancel to stop: the dialog says The open was cancelled. No project was added to this computer. If the project is already on this computer, the dialog says You already have this project on this computer. A project has one copy on each computer. Click Open it . If Door43 doesn't have a version the project uses, or it can't be downloaded, the dialog says This project cannot be opened. and names the version, for example Door43 does not have en_ult v91, which this project uses. No project is added. Click Ask for help if it keeps happening. Sync Select Sync with Door43 on the project card. The result says how many changes came from teammates and how many conflicts kept the newest version. If a teammate aligned the same verse after you did, the result names it, for example Titus 1:1: Ana's alignment replaced yours. Your links for that verse go back to the word bank. Open the verse in Align to check it. Nothing is merged automatically. If the internet is off, tC4 asks first. Not now sends nothing. If you aren't signed in, tC4 asks you to sign in to Door43. Versions the project uses A teammate may have moved the project to a newer source text or helps version, or this computer may not have one of the versions the project uses. Once the sync is done, tC4 downloads each version this computer lacks, with no question. While it downloads, the project card says, for example, Downloading es-419_twl v18, which this project uses. Versions left after this one: 4. A bar and Cancel download are beside it. The sync counts even if you cancel the download or a version can't be downloaded. Your changes were sent, and your teammates' changes were received. The result then says how many versions the project uses that are not on this computer, and why. For example, it says You cancelled the download of the versions this project uses. Or it names a version Door43 lacks: Door43 does not have es-419_tw v37, which this project uses. If one version can't be had, tC4 still downloads the others. Click Download in the result to get them now, or sync again later. The next sync downloads what is still missing. If the internet is turned off during the download, the download stops and the result says so. Story frame conflicts On Open Bible Stories projects, sync can find frames that two teammates both changed. The sync toast reads 1 frame conflict to review (or the count) with a Review link. The Home story card and the Translate top bar show an amber 1 conflict · Review chip. Project format and repaired files If a teammate's newer translationCore saved the project in a format this version cannot save to, sync stops and the toast names their format (for example 1.4 or 2 ). If format.json was deleted or damaged on Door43, tC4 puts this computer's copy back and the toast adds: A project file was deleted or damaged on Door43, so tC4 put your copy back. Good to know Bible and Open Bible Stories projects open and sync the same way. Don't copy project folders between computers by hand. tC4 refuses to open them, to protect the project. Related topics Share to Door43 Import a translationCore 3 project Export a project as a Scripture Burrito"}, {"id": "un-notes", "url": "un-notes", "title": "Translation notes and Translation Academy articles", "section": "Understand", "status": "shipped", "kind": "feature", "summary": "Translation notes explain hard phrases verse by verse. Each note can link to a Translation Academy article that teaches the translation issue behind it.", "where": "", "headings": ["Steps", "Good to know", "Related topics"], "labels": ["Abstract Nouns →", "INTRODUCTION", "Notes", "Show more", "Translation Academy →", "Understand", "VERSE 1"], "text": "Help › Understand Translation notes and Translation Academy articles Translation notes explain hard phrases verse by verse. Each note can link to a Translation Academy article that teaches the translation issue behind it. Many notes link to a Translation Academy article, a short lesson about a translation issue such as metaphors or abstract nouns. Steps In Understand , click a section to select it. In the helps panel, click Notes . Notes are grouped under headings such as INTRODUCTION and VERSE 1 . Point at a card to highlight its phrase in the passage. Click the article link on a note — it names the real Translation Academy article, for example Abstract Nouns → . The article opens under the notes with a close control. Close the article with ✕. Good to know The chapter introduction sits under INTRODUCTION . Click Show more to read all of it. Notes are in the project's checking language. If you see \"Shown from the English fallback\", the notes in that language aren't on this computer, so the English notes are shown. See Change the checking language . Article links name the Translation Academy article itself (for example Abstract Nouns → ), not a generic Translation Academy → label. If an article is missing, the panel says \"The pinned resources do not carry the article for this check.\" The note still shows. The same notes become check items in Check with Translation Notes . Related topics Read a passage before you translate Word links (Translation Words) Check with Translation Notes Change the checking language"}, {"id": "un-questions", "url": "un-questions", "title": "Translation questions", "section": "Understand", "status": "shipped", "kind": "feature", "summary": "Translation questions ask what the passage says. Use them to test whether your translation communicates the meaning.", "where": "", "headings": ["Steps", "Good to know", "Related topics"], "labels": ["Questions", "Translate", "Understand"], "text": "Help › Understand Translation questions Translation questions ask what the passage says. Use them to test whether your translation communicates the meaning. Ask them of someone who reads or hears your translation, and see whether their answers match. Steps In Understand , select a section. (The same tab is in the helps panel in Translate .) Click Questions . Read each question and its answer. Good to know If there are no questions, the tab says \"Nothing in this chapter.\" or \"Nothing for the verses in focus.\" They are useful after drafting and during community checking. Related topics Read a passage before you translate Community Checking preview The helps panel in Translate"}, {"id": "un-read-passage", "url": "un-read-passage", "title": "Read a passage before you translate", "section": "Understand", "status": "shipped", "kind": "feature", "summary": "Understand shows the source passage section by section, with its helps beside it and a box for your own comments.", "where": "", "headings": ["Read a passage", "Good to know", "Related topics"], "labels": ["Check", "Comment by", "GLT", "GST", "Notes", "Questions", "Section", "Translate", "ULT", "UST", "Understand", "Verse", "Words"], "text": "Help › Understand Read a passage before you translate Understand shows the source passage section by section, with its helps beside it and a box for your own comments. Many teams read and talk about a passage here before they draft it. Read a passage Open a book, then click Understand at the top. Pick a chapter in the book list on the left. Choose the source text with the ULT or UST tab. Projects with a Spanish checking language show GLT and GST instead. Next to Comment by , choose Section to read a section at a time, or Verse for one verse at a time. Read the helps on the right: Notes , Words , Questions and UST . Click a help card and the words it is about are highlighted in the passage. To keep a thought for later, type it in the comment box under the section. See Write a user comment . A small green dot on a section means you wrote a comment there. Good to know Sections follow the breaks in the source text, so a chapter can split differently in different gateway languages. Your choice of Section or Verse is shared with Translate. Understand shows the gateway texts. The Greek or Hebrew is in Translate and Check . Related topics Translation notes and Translation Academy articles Word links (Translation Words) Translation questions The simplified text tab Write a user comment Understand, Translate and Check — the three modes"}, {"id": "un-simplified", "url": "un-simplified", "title": "The simplified text tab", "section": "Understand", "status": "shipped", "kind": "feature", "summary": "The helps panel can show the meaning-based simplified text next to what you are reading.", "where": "", "headings": ["Steps", "Good to know", "Related topics"], "labels": ["Check", "GST", "Translate", "UST", "Understand"], "text": "Help › Understand The simplified text tab The helps panel can show the meaning-based simplified text next to what you are reading. It says the meaning plainly, which helps when the literal text is hard to follow. Steps In Understand or Translate , open the helps panel. Click UST . Some gateway languages name this tab differently, such as GST . Good to know Words in curly braces, such as {write this letter to you, Titus. I}, are part of the simplified text. They mark words added to make the meaning clear. You can also show the simplified text in the main source column with the UST source tab. In Check , Translation Notes and Translation Words show the simplified text as a third source card, with the quote highlighted. See Check with Translation Notes . Open Bible Stories have no simplified text, so there is no tab for it. Related topics Read a passage before you translate Source tabs — gateway texts and the original language"}, {"id": "un-user-comments", "url": "un-user-comments", "title": "Write a user comment", "section": "Understand", "status": "shipped", "kind": "feature", "summary": "Write down what a passage means, who is speaking, and what will be hard to translate. Your comments are saved with the project and shown in Translate.", "where": "", "headings": ["Steps", "Good to know", "Related topics"], "labels": ["Comment by", "Comments", "Section", "Translate", "Understand", "Verse"], "text": "Help › Understand Write a user comment Write down what a passage means, who is speaking, and what will be hard to translate. Your comments are saved with the project and shown in Translate. A comment is your own note about a section or verse, and it stays with the project. Steps In Understand , choose Section or Verse next to Comment by (1). Click in the box under the section or verse (2) and type your comment. Click outside the box. The comment is saved; watch the indicator at the top right. Later, in Translate , click Comments in the helps panel to read it. Good to know To change a comment, edit the text and click outside again. A saved comment can't be emptied: \"A saved user comment cannot be emptied — user comments are permanent in this version.\" Change the text instead. In Section view, if you wrote comments on single verses inside the section, tC4 suggests switching to Verse to see each one. These comments are separate from check comments. See Write a check comment . Related topics Read a passage before you translate The helps panel in Translate Write a check comment"}, {"id": "un-word-links", "url": "un-word-links", "title": "Word links (Translation Words)", "section": "Understand", "status": "shipped", "kind": "feature", "summary": "Word links point out the key biblical terms in the passage and link each to a Translation Words article that defines it.", "where": "", "headings": ["Steps", "Good to know", "Related topics"], "labels": ["Read the article →", "Understand", "Words"], "text": "Help › Understand Word links (Translation Words) Word links point out the key biblical terms in the passage and link each to a Translation Words article that defines it. Use them to translate key terms such as grace , apostle or righteous the same way every time. Steps In Understand , select a section. In the helps panel, click Words . Each card shows the first paragraph of the Translation Words article. Click a card to highlight every occurrence of that word across the section. Click Read the article → to open the full article under the cards. Close the article with ✕. Good to know The same released Links list drives Check with Translation Words . A term with no article in your checking language does not become a check item. Each card shows the word as it appears in the passage and the name of its article. The same terms become check items in Check with Translation Words . Related topics Read a passage before you translate Translation notes and Translation Academy articles Check with Translation Words"}, {"id": "wn-4.1", "url": "wn-4.1", "title": "What's new in 4.1", "section": "Release notes", "status": "shipped", "kind": "release", "summary": "Helps cards grouped by verse with named Translation Academy links and word intros, Clear alignments on Align, story frame conflicts on sync, project-format sync guards, and an app language that follows the computer until you choose.", "where": "", "headings": ["Start a Bible from files", "Drafting boxes", "About and other changes", "Helps", "Align", "Team sync and stories", "Language", "Added after 4.1.0-preview2", "Related topics"], "labels": ["1 conflict · Review", "1 frame conflict to review", "About translationCore", "Abstract Nouns →", "App language", "Blank", "Cancel", "Cancel download", "From USFM files", "From a Scripture Burrito", "How do you want to start?", "INTRODUCTION", "Leave without them", "New Bible", "Next", "Previous", "Read the article →", "Save", "Stay", "Third-party licenses", "Translation Academy →", "VERSE 1", "✓ Mark valid"], "text": "Help › Release notes What's new in 4.1 Helps cards grouped by verse with named Translation Academy links and word intros, Clear alignments on Align, story frame conflicts on sync, project-format sync guards, and an app language that follows the computer until you choose. translationCore 4.1 builds on the 4.0 rc.2 public beta. These are the main changes translators see. Start a Bible from files New Bible first asks How do you want to start? : Blank , From USFM files or From a Scripture Burrito . From USFM files, each file is checked first and its row says which book it adds, or why it can't be used. The form then suggests the versification the books fit. From a Scripture Burrito, the form fills in from the Burrito and lists its books. See Create a Bible project . Drafting boxes The section editor in Translate and the frame editor for stories are as tall as the source beside them. Longer text scrolls inside the box; drag its corner to make it taller. Highlights for a note or word now land on the right words where the gateway text joins two verses into one (a verse span). See Draft a section and Translate a story . About and other changes About translationCore shows the version number, the translationCore® trademark statement on the copyright line, and Third-party licenses . Menus and buttons in Hindi use a Devanagari font. File pickers open in the folder you used last. Helps Notes are grouped under verse headings. Cards sit under uppercase headings such as INTRODUCTION and VERSE 1 . The old NOTE / KEY WORD badges and verse pills are gone. Article links use the real name. A note that opens Translation Academy now says Abstract Nouns → (or the article's own title), not a generic Translation Academy → . Word cards show the first paragraph of the Translation Words article, then Read the article → . Click a word card to highlight every occurrence of that word across the section. Articles render cleanly — line breaks, superscripts and underlines from the source no longer show as raw tags. A floating close control sits above a divider. See The helps panel in Translate , Translation notes and Word links . Align Clear alignments sits on the same footer row as ✓ Mark valid . It asks first, then sends every placed word on that verse back to the word bank. Cards keep their original words. See Mark an alignment done . Team sync and stories Story frame conflicts. When two teammates both change a frame, sync reports 1 frame conflict to review , and Home and Translate show an amber 1 conflict · Review chip. Newer project format. If a teammate's newer translationCore saved the project in a format this version cannot save to, sync stops and names their format. Repaired Door43 files. If format.json was deleted or damaged on Door43, tC4 puts this computer's copy back and tells you. See Working as a team on several computers and Find a story on Home . Language App language follows the computer until you choose one in App language . After you pick, tC4 keeps your choice. Change checking language no longer offers the language already in use, and the confirm actions sit side by side. See Change the app language and Change the checking language . Added after 4.1.0-preview2 These changes came after the 4.1.0-preview2 test build. They are part of 4.1. Check: the source cards add the simplified text (UST, or GST such as the Indonesian one) beside the original and the literal text. See Check with Translation Notes . Check: only a mark saves the words you selected. Changing the words takes the mark away, Previous and Next wait until you mark or unselect, and leaving asks Stay or Leave without them . See Decide on a check item . Helps: the helps stay on the section, verse or story frame you clicked, also after Save . See The helps panel in Translate . Open a project from Door43: tC4 downloads the versions the project uses that your computer lacks, names each one, and Cancel stops the open cleanly. If a version can't be had, no project is added. See Working as a team on several computers . Sync with Door43: after the sync, tC4 downloads the versions the project uses that this computer lacks, with a line, a bar and Cancel download on the project card. A cancelled or failed download leaves the sync done, and the result says which versions are still missing and why. See Versions the project uses . Get source texts: a download keeps its line and Cancel until it ends; upgrades and their confirmations belong to the project where you started them. See Download source texts . Community Checking: with drop caps, verse 1 shows no small number beside the large chapter number. See Community Checking . Installers: a newer build replaces the installed translationCore 4 on macOS (it asks first) and Windows, keeping projects and settings. See Install and start tC4 . Related topics What's new in 4.0 rc.2 What's new in 4.0 rc.1 The helps panel in Translate Mark an alignment done Working as a team on several computers"}, {"id": "wn-add-a-project", "url": "wn-add-a-project", "title": "Sep 27, 2026 — one + Add a project button on Home", "section": "Release notes", "status": "shipped", "kind": "release", "summary": "Home now has one + Add a project button instead of three. It opens a dialog where you choose a new Bible, new Open Bible Stories, or an import. The Home heading is now Your projects.", "where": "", "headings": ["What changed", "Worth knowing", "Related topics"], "labels": ["+ Add a project", "Create Bible →", "Import a project", "New Bible", "New Open Bible Stories", "Your projects", "← Back"], "text": "Help › Release notes Sep 27, 2026 — one + Add a project button on Home Home now has one + Add a project button instead of three. It opens a dialog where you choose a new Bible, new Open Bible Stories, or an import. The Home heading is now Your projects . What changed Home's heading is now Your projects (it was \"Your Bibles\"). One blue + Add a project button replaces the separate buttons for a new Bible, new Open Bible Stories and import. + Add a project opens a dialog with three cards: New Bible , New Open Bible Stories and Import a project . See The Home screen . Each card opens the same form as before: Create a Bible project , Start an Open Bible Stories project , Import a project . Import step 1 has a ← Back button that returns to the three cards. With no projects yet, Home says \"No projects yet. Select + Add a project to start.\" Keyboard: Tab moves through the cards, Enter or Space opens one, and Esc closes the dialog. Worth knowing After a form closes, the keyboard focus doesn't return to + Add a project . The New Bible form can be taller than a small window. Scroll inside it to reach Create Bible → . Related topics alpha.1 — First public build alpha.2 — Check and align alpha.4 — Open and resume alpha.5 — Drafting for real alpha.6 — Suggestions and resources alpha.7 — Open Bible Stories What's new in 4.0 rc.1 What's new in 4.0 rc.2"}, {"id": "wn-alpha1", "url": "wn-alpha1", "title": "alpha.1 — First public build", "section": "Release notes", "status": "shipped", "kind": "release", "summary": "The first public snapshot of translationCore 4, a ground-up rewrite. Create a project and draft beside source texts.", "where": "", "headings": ["What arrived", "Worth knowing", "Related topics"], "labels": [], "text": "Help › Release notes alpha.1 — First public build The first public snapshot of translationCore 4, a ground-up rewrite. Create a project and draft beside source texts. What arrived Draft : create a project, choose source texts, and draft beside them. See Start a new Bible project . Check and Align : work started, entering review. Format : every project is a Scripture Burrito. Worth knowing translationCore 3 is a separate program. Projects can now be imported (see Import a project ), but tC3 versions do not carry over. Related topics alpha.2 — Check and align alpha.4 — Open and resume alpha.5 — Drafting for real alpha.6 — Suggestions and resources alpha.7 — Open Bible Stories Sep 27, 2026 — one + Add a project button on Home What's new in 4.0 rc.1 What's new in 4.0 rc.2"}, {"id": "wn-alpha2", "url": "wn-alpha2", "title": "alpha.2 — Check and align", "section": "Release notes", "status": "shipped", "kind": "release", "summary": "Check a whole book with Translation Notes and Translation Words, and align verses to the original language.", "where": "", "headings": ["What arrived", "Related topics"], "labels": [], "text": "Help › Release notes alpha.2 — Check and align Check a whole book with Translation Notes and Translation Words, and align verses to the original language. What arrived Check with Translation Notes and Translation Words , against resources pinned at exact versions. Align : link each word of a verse to the original language. Editing a verse marks its alignment for review. Related topics alpha.1 — First public build alpha.4 — Open and resume alpha.5 — Drafting for real alpha.6 — Suggestions and resources alpha.7 — Open Bible Stories Sep 27, 2026 — one + Add a project button on Home What's new in 4.0 rc.1 What's new in 4.0 rc.2"}, {"id": "wn-alpha4", "url": "wn-alpha4", "title": "alpha.4 — Open and resume", "section": "Release notes", "status": "shipped", "kind": "release", "summary": "Resume where you left off after a restart, checkpoints on mode changes, and the new design for Understand, Translate and Check.", "where": "", "headings": ["What arrived", "Worth knowing", "Related topics"], "labels": [], "text": "Help › Release notes alpha.4 — Open and resume Resume where you left off after a restart, checkpoints on mode changes, and the new design for Understand, Translate and Check. What arrived Resume across a restart: Home's Resume card returns you to the book and chapter. Checkpoints : saved when you switch mode or leave a project. \"Checkpoint not saved\" offers Retry. The Understand, Translate and Check workflow on the new design. Align rebuilt as a Check tool (with gateway and original modes ). A progress bar while a project opens. Worth knowing This build shipped no English scripture; later builds include it. Related topics alpha.1 — First public build alpha.2 — Check and align alpha.5 — Drafting for real alpha.6 — Suggestions and resources alpha.7 — Open Bible Stories Sep 27, 2026 — one + Add a project button on Home What's new in 4.0 rc.1 What's new in 4.0 rc.2"}, {"id": "wn-alpha5", "url": "wn-alpha5", "title": "alpha.5 — Drafting for real", "section": "Release notes", "status": "shipped", "kind": "release", "summary": "Draft a whole section at a time, place verse numbers, join verses into spans, format paragraphs and poetry, and read a passage with helps.", "where": "", "headings": ["What arrived", "Related topics"], "labels": [], "text": "Help › Release notes alpha.5 — Drafting for real Draft a whole section at a time, place verse numbers, join verses into spans, format paragraphs and poetry, and read a passage with helps. What arrived Draft a section and place verse numbers . Verse spans such as 9-10 (Bible only). Paragraph and poetry formatting . The Greek or Hebrew text beside ULT and UST in Translate. Understand with notes, questions and user comments . The English resources ship with the app, so you can work offline from the first start. A Windows build. Related topics alpha.1 — First public build alpha.2 — Check and align alpha.4 — Open and resume alpha.6 — Suggestions and resources alpha.7 — Open Bible Stories Sep 27, 2026 — one + Add a project button on Home What's new in 4.0 rc.1 What's new in 4.0 rc.2"}, {"id": "wn-alpha6", "url": "wn-alpha6", "title": "alpha.6 — Suggestions and resources", "section": "Release notes", "status": "shipped", "kind": "release", "summary": "Alignment suggestions, check comments and bookmarks, resource upgrades, and a guided fix for missing resources.", "where": "", "headings": ["What arrived", "Worth knowing", "Related topics"], "labels": [], "text": "Help › Release notes alpha.6 — Suggestions and resources Alignment suggestions, check comments and bookmarks, resource upgrades, and a guided fix for missing resources. What arrived Alignment suggestions : off by default; you confirm or reject each. Mark an alignment done . Check comments and bookmarks . Update pinned helps with Check for updates. Fix a missing resource . Resume returns to the mode you last used. Installers for macOS (.pkg) and Windows (.exe). One Section/Verse switch in Translate; the helps panel follows the verses in focus. Worth knowing Windows needs the Microsoft Visual C++ runtime. Related topics alpha.1 — First public build alpha.2 — Check and align alpha.4 — Open and resume alpha.5 — Drafting for real alpha.7 — Open Bible Stories Sep 27, 2026 — one + Add a project button on Home What's new in 4.0 rc.1 What's new in 4.0 rc.2"}, {"id": "wn-alpha7", "url": "wn-alpha7", "title": "alpha.7 — Open Bible Stories", "section": "Release notes", "status": "shipped", "kind": "release", "summary": "Create an Open Bible Stories project, translate stories frame by frame with pictures, read them with helps, and check them.", "where": "", "headings": ["What arrived", "Worth knowing", "Related topics"], "labels": [], "text": "Help › Release notes alpha.7 — Open Bible Stories Create an Open Bible Stories project, translate stories frame by frame with pictures, read them with helps, and check them. What arrived Start an Open Bible Stories project and find stories on Home . Translate a story frame by frame beside the pictures. Understand a story with notes, word links and questions per frame. Check a story with Translation Notes and Translation Words. The OBS resources and pictures ship with the app. A failed save retries one attempt at a time. Worth knowing The release notes said export was not built yet. It arrived in rc.1: see What's new in 4.0 rc.1 . Related topics alpha.1 — First public build alpha.2 — Check and align alpha.4 — Open and resume alpha.5 — Drafting for real alpha.6 — Suggestions and resources Sep 27, 2026 — one + Add a project button on Home What's new in 4.0 rc.1 What's new in 4.0 rc.2"}, {"id": "wn-rc1", "url": "wn-rc1", "title": "What's new in 4.0 rc.1", "section": "Release notes", "status": "shipped", "kind": "release", "summary": "Get your work out and bring existing work in: export a book as PDF, USFM or a Scripture Burrito, export stories as Markdown, PDF or a burrito, and import translationCore 3, USFM and Scripture Burrito files. Plus project Settings on the Home card, new books opening in Understand, English helps v91 in the box, and Spacing and Paper size for printing.", "where": "", "headings": ["What arrived", "Export", "Import", "Also new", "Worth knowing", "Related topics"], "labels": ["+ Add a project", "Check", "Community Checking", "Details", "Export", "Export PDF", "Import a project", "Imported", "License", "Missing verses", "Open →", "Scripture Burrito (.zip)", "Settings", "Story Markdown (.zip)", "USFM, aligned", "USFM, plain", "Understand"], "text": "Help › Release notes What's new in 4.0 rc.1 Get your work out and bring existing work in: export a book as PDF, USFM or a Scripture Burrito, export stories as Markdown, PDF or a burrito, and import translationCore 3, USFM and Scripture Burrito files. Plus project Settings on the Home card, new books opening in Understand, English helps v91 in the box, and Spacing and Paper size for printing. What arrived Export All exports start from Check › Community Checking › Open → › Export . When the file is saved, the panel says \"Saved <file name>\". Bible book: Export PDF , USFM, aligned , USFM, plain and Scripture Burrito (.zip) . See Export a book as PDF , Export a book as USFM and Export a project as a Scripture Burrito . Open Bible Stories: Export PDF (with or without pictures), Story Markdown (.zip) and Scripture Burrito (.zip) . See Export stories as PDF or Markdown . A book with nothing drafted isn't printed. tC4 tells you to draft at least one verse first. Import + Add a project › Import a project makes a new project from a translationCore 3 project, USFM files or a Scripture Burrito. See Import a project . The review shows the books, three checks ( License , Details , Missing verses ) and what carries over. A missing language code or a Bible name already in use must be fixed before you can import. Damaged files are refused with a reason and a code, and nothing is created. See Messages that protect your project . The new card has an Imported badge. Also new Settings on the card: click Settings on a project's Home card. See Project settings . A cleaner top bar. Cards say \"Only on this computer\" or where the project is shared. See The Home screen . New books open in Understand , so you read before you draft. After that a book reopens in the mode you used last. See The three modes . English helps v91 come with the app , so a new project needs no download. See What comes with the app . Moving between a Bible and a stories project no longer confuses their notes and words. See Check a story . Page setup for printing: spacing and paper size for Bible books, and picture layout for stories. See Community Checking preview and Story preview and export . Window › Reload (Ctrl+R) brings the app back without quitting. A screen that can't be drawn shows \"This screen could not be shown.\" with Back to Home . See If the app closed unexpectedly . Resume returns to the last mode , including the checking tool you were using. See Resume where you left off . Check for updates offers only the texts your books need. A New Testament project isn't offered the Hebrew text. See Update scripture texts . Changing the checking language keeps the checks that still match. The rest come back as work; nothing is deleted. See Change the checking language . translationCore 3 imports ask whether to look up the resource versions on Door43 or use the ones on this computer. See Import a project . Worth knowing After you join verses into a span, the book's percentage can drop, and the Resume banner can still name the old verse. The \"… was imported\" message doesn't close by itself. Close it with its ✕. In USFM exports, the \\h , \\toc and \\mt lines carry the book code (for example TIT), not the book name. On Linux, the save window's Save and Cancel buttons can be off-screen. Press Enter to save, or Esc to cancel. The same language can be named two ways, for example \"Español (Latinoamérica)\" in lists and \"Spanish (Latin American)\" in sentences. Related topics alpha.1 — First public build alpha.2 — Check and align alpha.4 — Open and resume alpha.5 — Drafting for real alpha.6 — Suggestions and resources alpha.7 — Open Bible Stories Sep 27, 2026 — one + Add a project button on Home What's new in 4.0 rc.2"}, {"id": "wn-rc2", "url": "wn-rc2", "title": "What's new in 4.0 rc.2", "section": "Release notes", "status": "shipped", "kind": "release", "summary": "The public beta. A preparing window on first start, numbered repeated words and multi-word placing in Align, drag-select in Check, and Previous and Next beside the marking buttons. Updated October 20: poetry verses open whole, and alignments follow their words.", "where": "", "headings": ["Starting tC4", "Align", "Check", "Translate", "Also new since rc.1", "rc.2 update — October 20, 2026", "Translate", "Align", "Export", "Team sync", "Import", "Related topics"], "labels": ["About translationCore", "Add a book", "Add books from USFM files", "App language", "Help and guides", "Invalid", "Next", "Previous", "Report a problem", "To do", "Use the internet", "Your translation", "✓ Mark valid"], "text": "Help › Release notes What's new in 4.0 rc.2 The public beta. A preparing window on first start, numbered repeated words and multi-word placing in Align, drag-select in Check, and Previous and Next beside the marking buttons. Updated October 20: poetry verses open whole, and alignments follow their words. 4.0.0-rc.2 is the public beta. These are the main changes since rc.1. Starting tC4 A preparing window on the first start. \"Preparing translationCore4\" counts the bundled resources, for example \"Preparing resources: 2 of 16\", and then tC4 opens on Home. Later starts are much faster. If the download is damaged, tC4 says so and quits without changing anything. See Install and start tC4 . Align Repeated words are numbered. A word that comes more than once in the verse shows which copy it is: de¹, de², de³. The source cards use the same numbers. Place several words at once. Ctrl-click (Cmd-click on a Mac) or Shift-click words in the word bank, then click or drag them onto one card. Cards keep verse order. Words on a card always show in the order they come in your verse, wherever you drop them. See Link your words to the source . Check Drag to select words. Drag across Your translation to select whole words. ✓ Mark valid saves the selection, and it is still there when you come back to the item. This works in Translation Notes and Translation Words. See Decide on a check item . Previous and Next beside the marks. Previous and Next are now on the same row as ✓ Mark valid , Invalid and To do , in Check and in Align. Translate The Draft section button is always visible , as a small rounded button on each section. See Draft a section . Also new since rc.1 Translation Words checks use released Links. English ships en_twl v91 beside en_tw v91. Gateway downloads install their own _twl when published; otherwise English Links pair with that language's articles. Books not in the Links release have no tW check. Missing-article rows are hidden. See Check with Translation Words and What comes with the app . The internet is off until you turn it on. A step that needs the internet asks \"Turn on the internet?\" first. tC4 then uses the internet until you switch off Use the internet in the account menu, or close tC4. See Working offline . Add books from USFM files to a project you already have: Add a book › Add books from USFM files . Each file becomes a book. See Add a book . Sharing on Door43 uses the same question. It also says you'll review the destination and books before anything is uploaded. See Share to Door43 . The Stories list keeps its titles and percentages while you draft a story. See Translate a story . Project settings says \"1 book\" instead of \"1 books\". Account menu: Help and guides (offline), About translationCore , App language (nine languages) and Report a problem . See The Home screen and Change the app language . Verse spans count in the drafted percentage , and joining verses across a paragraph no longer leaves a blank line inside the span. See Make or break a verse span . Resume names the verse or span you were on after a span change. See Resume where you left off . Place verse numbers supports click-then-click to move a number you already placed. See Place verse numbers . Understand / Translate / Check stay centered when Saved flips to Unsaved. See The three modes . You can save a section after deleting all of its text (placeholder verses). See Draft a section . Helps articles render quotes and bold cleanly; UST/GST verse numbers are superscript. See Read the notes and Simplified text . rc.2 update — October 20, 2026 rc.2 was rebuilt on October 20 with these changes. The version number stays 4.0.0-rc.2. Translate Poetry verses open whole. The Verse and Section editors show every line of a poetry verse, each indented one tab per level, with paragraph and chunk breaks as blank lines. Saving no longer leaves old text behind. See Paragraphs and poetry . All verses of poetry books show. Verses that start on a poetry line, for example most of Psalms and Genesis 49, now appear in Translate and Align. Verses with nested alignments show all their words, for example \"the heavens\" in Genesis 1:1. Align The word bank always matches the verse text as it is now, and a card click places only the words shown as selected. See Link your words to the source . A link stays on its word when you edit the verse. Deleting a linked word removes its link. A change that leaves it unclear which copy of a repeated word you kept flags the verse for review. See Editing a verse you already checked or aligned . One rule decides which alignments need review, for the verse list, the notice, ✓ Mark valid, the translationCore 3 import and the aligned export. Source cards follow your project's original-language text, as in translationCore 3. Verses imported with alignments are kept as imported and show \"This verse already holds an alignment\". See Open the Align tool . Suggestions use the same words as the word bank, and work better for Hebrew. Export USFM, aligned names any verse whose alignment needs review and is not in the file: \"Your alignment of these verses is not in the file\". See Export a book as USFM . Team sync The sync result names each verse where a teammate's newer alignment replaced yours. See Working as a team . After a sync, a project that was open shows a plain message asking you to close and open it again, and Home always lets you leave. Import A Scripture Burrito without a versification file imports without \"Checkpoint not saved\" errors. Related topics alpha.1 — First public build alpha.2 — Check and align alpha.4 — Open and resume alpha.5 — Drafting for real alpha.6 — Suggestions and resources alpha.7 — Open Bible Stories Sep 27, 2026 — one + Add a project button on Home What's new in 4.0 rc.1"}]};
